WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:22.040
So I want to continue talking about this basic
story or sub personality or game or map.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:31.700
So what I’ve discussed with you so far is
that there are different ways that you can

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:41.480
construe the objects and territories that
you inhabit, and that modern people generally

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:51.390
think that the world they inhabit is one that's
defined by science, but I would say - I would

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:57.350
say actually it depends on which scientist
you mean because I don't believe that the

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:02.980
worldview that emerges as a logical consequence
of Darwinism is the same worldview that emerges

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:10.111
as a logical consequence of Newtonian presuppositions
and you know the reason I’m bringing Newton

00:01:10.111 --> 00:01:15.010
into it is because we still think in Newtonian
terms and it’s been more than a hundred

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:20.700
years or about a hundred years I guess, since
Newton’s model was expanded by quantum physics,

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.470
but no one thinks that way because you can't
think that way and besides that, the reason

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:30.360
you don't think that way is because at the
level of resolution that you exist, things

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:38.310
behave in a Newtonian manner so it’s in
accordance with some of our natural perceptions.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:46.390
Now, I started thinking this way for a variety
of reasons but I was influenced later by a

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:51.650
guy named Gibson - JJ Gibson, who wrote a
book called “An Ecological Approach to Visual

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:52.830
Perception.”

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:59.210
And he made the case, quite strongly, that
what you directly perceive is the implication

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.730
of situations and objects.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:07.510
So for example, I suppose you've all seen
the famous visual cliff experiment with babies

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:17.190
where if you put a piece of glass across an
open space and the space is evidently deep,

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:21.700
the baby won’t cross the glass and so there's
some evidence there for relatively innate,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.010
not only depth processing.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:29.240
It’s not just depth processing, right, because
in order to not crawl off that, you also have

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:34.599
to perceive that - well Gibson would say that
it’s a falling off place, and he would say

00:02:34.599 --> 00:02:38.959
that's actually what you perceive is a falling
off place and then maybe you can extract out

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:47.370
the idea of cliff, but that isn't what you
perceive and you know, the sense you have

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:53.450
when a bear is chasing you that’s dangerous,
that doesn't seem to be merely psychological

00:02:53.450 --> 00:03:00.099
in a sense like it’s not merely, in a sense,
not - it’s not less real than perceiving

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:01.690
the bear as an object.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:09.400
An object that eats you is not the same object
as an object that remains neutral to you or

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:15.520
positive, and it seems quite clear, and this
is where the Darwinian issue I think arises,

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:25.900
that you have to evaluate with your perception
because otherwise you're too slow.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:34.670
So anyways, I developed this diagram as a
consequence of thinking about these things

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:41.300
for a long time, and the diagram is predicated
on the idea that when you begin to act in

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:47.930
the world, you're inside a motivational frame,
let’s say, and that motivational frame can

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:57.810
be instantiated or put into practice or put
into place by any number of underlying motivational

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:05.750
systems, some of them the more basic motivational
systems that we share with animals going far

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:12.349
back down the evolutionary chain and some
of them more complex mixtures of emotional

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:21.919
or motivational drives that been organized
across lengthy periods of times in social

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:23.789
circumstances.

00:04:23.789 --> 00:04:33.020
So for example, if you're at a bar and you're
looking for someone, it’s not really reasonable

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:36.820
to say - we’ll take the simple case.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:44.580
It’s not really reasonable to say that your
behavior is motivated by sex because that's

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:50.170
what happens only if you're actually in a
sexual act.

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:54.730
Before that, the motivations are multiple
and they have to be because otherwise you

00:04:54.730 --> 00:05:00.580
wouldn't have the sophistication to begin
a conversation with anyone.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:07.850
So to think of that as driven in some sense
by sexuality means either to consider a much

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:14.660
more expanded notion of sexuality or to not
consider it driven in that manner.

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:21.580
Now, I think it’s reasonable to make the
presumption that you're motivated to move

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:26.050
ahead and we know that for a variety of reasons.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:30.080
Partly that’s because if you look at the
brain at the level of the hypothalamus which

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:40.410
is a very, very archaic and primordial subsystem
in the brain, then it’s roughly divided

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:46.550
into two parts and one part is responsible
for a lot of the things we consider basic

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:53.220
motivations, and the other part is responsible
for exploration.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:06:04.340
So the dopaminergic tracts whose operation
underlies movement forward and incentive reward

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:09.229
and what you generally experience as the sorts
of positive emotion associated with hope,

00:06:09.229 --> 00:06:14.630
and excitement, and curiosity, that system
is instantiated in the hypothalamus, and a

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:18.030
lot of the hypothalamus is devoted to it.

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:29.100
So, as a very primordial system whose operation
appears to be a default.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:37.979
It’s a default operation if none of the
other motivational systems need current attention.

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:41.600
Well the other default I suppose is sleep
or rest and you can certainly see that in

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.960
animals because, you know, if you have a cat
you’ll notice - of course they’re nocturnal,

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.639
but they'll sleep a large part of the day
and so do dogs.

00:06:50.639 --> 00:06:55.051
So, you know, if you're not looking for a
partner, if you don't want to play, if you're

00:06:55.051 --> 00:07:00.850
not hungry, not thirsty, and so on, not under
the grip of any direct biological necessity

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:09.990
let’s say, then your default position is
to rest or sleep or to explore, and the thing

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:12.830
about the exploratory circuit is it’s really
old.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:14.720
It’s as old as pain.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.500
It’s as old as lust.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:17.720
It’s as old as anger.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:19.310
It’s as old as hunger.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:20.680
It’s as old as thirst.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:22.340
Like it’s a really, really old system.

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:30.080
It’s a very primordial system, and human
beings are quite hyper exploratory, at least

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:36.960
collectively as you can tell because we keep
inventing new things and exchanging new modes

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.770
of being.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:46.770
So, you can say in some sense that you have
a drive to engage with the world and to expand

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:49.880
your domain of knowledge.

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:58.080
And the reason that you have that is because
the human race has gambled on that as a sufficient

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:04.230
means of ensuring adaptation into the future,
you know, and it’s a strange one because

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:06.560
most animals are very, very conservative.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:12.050
In fact, most human societies are very, very
conservative so it’s certainly possible

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:16.410
for even relatively modern human societies
- so let’s say those that have arisen in

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:21.940
the last fifty thousand years - to remain
relatively unchanged for periods during that

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:23.590
time of up to ten thousand years.

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:29.919
That seems to be what happened, for example,
with regards to the original inhabitants of

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:35.839
Australia and so it’s statis that’s the
norm, not transformation, and you know, even

00:08:35.839 --> 00:08:41.919
societies that we feel were very dynamic like
let’s say Ancient Egypt still there are

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:47.220
indications in the archaeological records
of relatively unchanged customs and practices

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.920
for periods that you would count in the thousands.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:58.600
So, most animals and many societies bet that
just doing the same thing over and over is

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:07.959
gonna do the trick, and human beings - well
that isn't what we’re like man.

00:09:07.959 --> 00:09:10.489
God only knows how that originally came about,
you know.

00:09:10.489 --> 00:09:18.920
It was the conjunction of many, many processes
and forces, and it would be very, very difficult

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:23.779
to tell a complete causal story, but anyways
here we are.

00:09:23.779 --> 00:09:32.670
Now, it’s reasonable to think of us as motivated
to move forward and motivated to explore,

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:38.910
and so that's the fundamental motivation for
engaging with the world apart from anything

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:41.640
that’s a strictly lower level biological.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:48.850
And while we’re doing that, it’s also
customary for us to look at the world in terms

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:54.579
of its utility, and I would say with human
beings that it’s a very tool based utility

00:09:54.579 --> 00:09:58.059
because you know, our brain is part of our
body.

00:09:58.059 --> 00:10:01.899
Our brain is adapted to our body and our body
is adapted to our brain.

00:10:01.899 --> 00:10:05.540
Obviously they coevolved and so the kind of
brain we have is the sort of brain that a

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:12.810
creature that stands upright and can manipulate
the world has, and we’ll see some evidence

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:13.810
of that later.

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:19.670
But ebcause we’re so handy, we can speak
with our hands and we can manipulate things

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:23.639
with our hands and we can transform things
with our hands and we can tear things apart

00:10:23.639 --> 00:10:30.899
and we can hunt and we can plant and there's
an endless number of possibilities for hand

00:10:30.899 --> 00:10:32.220
utilization.

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:38.839
The world appears to us arrayed out as tools,
roughly speaking, and a tool would be something

00:10:38.839 --> 00:10:46.699
- it’s hard to get the word exactly right
because I like tool because tool implies something

00:10:46.699 --> 00:10:57.170
that you can use whereas Gibson would have
talked about - what did he call it?

00:10:57.170 --> 00:11:00.440
I can’t remember.

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:03.459
It’ll come to me in a minute.

00:11:03.459 --> 00:11:09.899
The word reflects the fact that the world
is presenting itself to you as something you

00:11:09.899 --> 00:11:10.899
can use.

00:11:10.899 --> 00:11:15.329
Let me just look that up for a second.

00:11:15.329 --> 00:11:17.839
That’s very annoying.

00:11:17.839 --> 00:11:20.360
Oh well, whatever.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:24.899
So naturally when you lay out the world, it
lays itself out into things that are going

00:11:24.899 --> 00:11:32.309
to be useful for you, things that are going
to get in your way, and then things that have

00:11:32.309 --> 00:11:34.519
neither property.

00:11:34.519 --> 00:11:40.749
Under most circumstances, the things that
have neither property are the overwhelming

00:11:40.749 --> 00:11:48.040
majority, and that's partly how you manage
to process the world in all of its complexity

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:56.199
with your relatively narrow processing capacity.

00:11:56.199 --> 00:12:00.679
Most things have no significance, and they
have no significance within this very very

00:12:00.679 --> 00:12:06.899
focal frame which is if you remember the hierarchy
diagram, it’s like in that hierarchy diagram,

00:12:06.899 --> 00:12:10.199
most of the time you’re operating at the
level of motor interaction with the world.

00:12:10.199 --> 00:12:14.160
You’re actually engaged with the world either
through articulation, but forget about that

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.550
for now, or you're actually engaged moving
in the world and moving it around and interacting

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:20.579
with it.

00:12:20.579 --> 00:12:29.240
It’s as if you're viewing the world through
a series of concentric lenses and those concentric

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:37.870
lenses exclude and then the highest resolution
lens, the one that enables you to focus closest,

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:44.149
is the one that helps you highlight those
few things in the vast perceptual world of

00:12:44.149 --> 00:12:50.810
nothingness that seem to be critical for that
particular operation.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:53.449
So now, you can see that.

00:12:53.449 --> 00:13:00.089
I mean the most dramatic example of that - I
was really personally thrilled about Dan Simon’s

00:13:00.089 --> 00:13:07.859
gorilla experiments because I’d been thinking
about this for a long time before that experiment

00:13:07.859 --> 00:13:10.449
came out and it was the clearest demonstration
I had seen.

00:13:10.449 --> 00:13:13.819
I mean, there's a lot of demonstrations like
that now but it was the clearest demonstration

00:13:13.819 --> 00:13:21.239
I’d ever seen that indeed most of what you're
perceptual systems do is exclude.

00:13:21.239 --> 00:13:27.939
Now what that means - this is the tricky thing
is that what that means is that wrapped up

00:13:27.939 --> 00:13:35.499
in what you exclude is the entire - is all
of being, virtually and that's a very tricky

00:13:35.499 --> 00:13:41.680
problem because when you're excluding all
of being, in some sense you're setting its

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:43.949
value to zero.

00:13:43.949 --> 00:13:49.020
You say, “Well except for those few things
that I’m concentrating on now, everything

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:52.759
else is zero and it’s zero and unchanging.”

00:13:52.759 --> 00:13:57.239
And the problem with that is that lots of
times that’s not right.

00:13:57.239 --> 00:14:03.059
Now it’s right enough, luckily enough, and
I suppose this is a consequence of evolution

00:14:03.059 --> 00:14:04.339
too.

00:14:04.339 --> 00:14:11.600
We’re fast enough or slow enough, depending
on how you look at it so that we can assume

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:16.619
constancy across some span of time under some
conditions.

00:14:16.619 --> 00:14:21.360
Now, from a philosophical perspective that's
inadequate.

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:27.399
Some of you might have heard of the phrase
“The scandal of induction.”

00:14:27.399 --> 00:14:35.180
Well, it’s a pointer to a very profound
philosophical problem and the philosophical

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:41.209
problem is how do you know the same thing
that happened before is going to happen next?

00:14:41.209 --> 00:14:47.449
And the answer to that is generally you don't
know, and it’s a very tricky issue to come

00:14:47.449 --> 00:14:50.899
to terms with you know.

00:14:50.899 --> 00:14:57.549
I think you can think of things as you know,
if you think about a hierarchy of resolution

00:14:57.549 --> 00:14:58.549
again.

00:14:58.549 --> 00:15:04.079
I think you can think of things as very, rather
predictably, in their unpredictability.

00:15:04.079 --> 00:15:08.779
So for example, there's some indication that
protons decay.

00:15:08.779 --> 00:15:12.879
But they decay over such a long time frame
that the probability that that’s going to

00:15:12.879 --> 00:15:17.859
affect us is virtually zero, and there's other
things that we interact with quite regularly

00:15:17.859 --> 00:15:28.290
like the sun that are stable across timeframes
that make it virtually ignorable.

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:33.410
Well I’ll tell you a story about that, and
this is a good story to indicate how everything

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:35.910
is nested in what you ignore.

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:40.149
So, part of this story is fictional, but part
of it isn’t.

00:15:40.149 --> 00:15:46.600
So, one time when I was in graduate school
in Quebec, in Montreal, I was typing on my

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:56.499
pre-hard drive computer and it stopped, and
you know computers do that and so what happens

00:15:56.499 --> 00:15:58.119
when your computer stops?

00:15:58.119 --> 00:16:01.749
Well, the first thing that happens is it depends
on what you're working on.

00:16:01.749 --> 00:16:04.360
That’s the critical issue right?

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:10.939
So what happens is that you have a reaction
that's proportionate to the deemed importance

00:16:10.939 --> 00:16:14.889
of what it was that you’re working on and
there's going to be an association between

00:16:14.889 --> 00:16:19.500
that and deadlines, and there's going to be
an association between that and amount written

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:23.309
that you think you might have lost, and then.

00:16:23.309 --> 00:16:28.850
So there's, there's - because what you're
doing is nested in a hierarchy, you know,

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:34.799
I’m writing a sentence to write a paragraph
to write a essay to hand the essay in to fulfill

00:16:34.799 --> 00:16:41.679
a requirement of class to graduate, etc. all
the way up the nested hierarchy.

00:16:41.679 --> 00:16:49.419
You're going to be upset in proportion to
the upset - or to the cost of this failure

00:16:49.419 --> 00:16:51.220
in terms of that entire structure.

00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:55.190
So you do a rough estimate eh, because you
don't really know and generally you don't

00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:59.130
assume the worst although you might immediately
because that's the sort of situation where

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:02.679
someone will go - they’ll get angry, or
I will anyways.

00:17:02.679 --> 00:17:08.230
I’ll pound my first on the - and then I’ll
curse Microsoft usually or whoever happened

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:13.150
to make the computer, and then I’ll remember
I can usually fix computers if there's something

00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:16.959
wrong with them and that probably all it needs
to do is to be rebooted and so forth.

00:17:16.959 --> 00:17:23.620
And so what happens to begin with is a burst
of emotion and the reason for that is that

00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:29.340
the significance of the unexpected event cannot
be easily constrained, and that's because

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:31.990
there's so many things that might gone wrong.

00:17:31.990 --> 00:17:38.639
Now, you know, because the worst case scenario
is maybe your computer is dead, and then that’s

00:17:38.639 --> 00:17:43.549
a problem for all sorts of reasons we don't
have to go into, and it’s not just a technical

00:17:43.549 --> 00:17:44.549
problem right?

00:17:44.549 --> 00:17:49.600
It’s also a political, and social, and economic
problem, weirdly enough, because then when

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:53.450
you go buy a computer you have to figure out,
well who’s the manufacturer and where’s

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:56.610
the manufacturing done, and what's a high
quality computer, and it’s like good luck

00:17:56.610 --> 00:18:00.880
figuring that out because they change every
month so you just can't keep track of it.

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:05.580
So it opens up a whole rat’s nest, or hornet’s
nest of problems - or a pit of snakes which

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:07.789
is a better way of thinking about it.

00:18:07.789 --> 00:18:08.789
Okay so, fine.

00:18:08.789 --> 00:18:11.549
So, my computer’s dead so I try to reboot
it.

00:18:11.549 --> 00:18:15.620
Doesn't reboot, and so I think I don't know,
maybe it’s burned out or maybe there's something

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:16.620
wrong with the plug.

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:21.620
So I plug a lamp into the same outlet and
I try to turn on the lamp and the lamp doesn't

00:18:21.620 --> 00:18:22.770
go on, so then I think aha!

00:18:22.770 --> 00:18:27.470
I blew a fuse because this place had fuses
so fine.

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:31.120
So I go to the fuse box, and it’s daytime
so it’s not a problem, and I look at the

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:35.530
fuses and there's nothing wrong with the fuses
and so I think well I’ll go down to the

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:40.950
corner store and get a cigarette because I
smoked at that time, and I’ll think about

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:41.950
it on the way.

00:18:41.950 --> 00:18:44.240
So I go outside and all the street lights
are off.

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:45.870
So the whole city’s come to a halt.

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:47.809
I think, oh there’s a power outage.

00:18:47.809 --> 00:18:51.799
It’s like yeah there was a power outage,
and here's what happened.

00:18:51.799 --> 00:18:54.430
A solar flare emerged from the sun, surfacing.

00:18:54.430 --> 00:19:01.240
It was a whopping big one, and a solar flare
like that sends out a wind, essentially, of

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:08.600
charged particles that come zooming towards
the Earth and we’re protected from them

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:15.659
for a variety of reasons, including the fact
that the Earth is magnetic and for complicated

00:19:15.659 --> 00:19:20.960
reasons, that helps protect us from cosmic
rays which is really a good thing as far as

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:21.960
I’m concerned.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:27.320
But now and then those things are so powerful
that they’ll - well here’s an example.

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:31.470
If you have a hydrogen bomb, a big one, and
you detonate it in the middle of North America

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:36.559
in the atmosphere, high up, you’ll wipe
out all the electronics in the entire continent

00:19:36.559 --> 00:19:40.940
permanently because what happens is that when
the bomb goes off, there's an electromagnetic

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:46.470
pulse and that produces an electric current
that runs through the wiring, and that'll

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:53.029
be of - very brief obviously but of sufficient
intensity to fry the electronics.

00:19:53.029 --> 00:19:55.230
So, well that's what the sun is.

00:19:55.230 --> 00:20:00.050
The sun is a massive hydrogen bomb and so
now and then it goes off with the force of,

00:20:00.050 --> 00:20:06.179
you know, millions of hydrogen bombs and then
we get the pulse radiating towards us and

00:20:06.179 --> 00:20:11.940
then it sets a current in the power grid which
what happened here and takes out the whole

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:12.940
power grid.

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:14.389
That's what happened at that point.

00:20:14.389 --> 00:20:19.120
The Quebec power grid was down for awhile,
and it was because - so that’s why my computer

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:24.080
crashed is because the sun wasn't as stable
as I thought it was.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:31.379
That gives you an example of just how interconnected
things are and how much you're taking for

00:20:31.379 --> 00:20:35.440
granted when you make anything invisible.

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:39.600
I would say that part of what happens in a
civilized society - we’ll say a civilized

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:45.799
and productive society - is that there are
rules of conduct in place and there are effective

00:20:45.799 --> 00:20:51.159
processes in place so that most of the time
you can rely on things being like they were

00:20:51.159 --> 00:20:52.250
yesterday.

00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:59.000
So for example, our power supply is unbelievably
reliable given that it’s so intrinsically

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:00.180
unstable.

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:04.700
People have to beetle around nonstop just
to keep the power on, but they do and so we

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:11.190
can sit here and ignore it, and we can concentrate
on something more arcane and much more focused

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:17.491
but it’s also because it’s a relatively
peaceful society and we have relatively peaceful

00:21:17.491 --> 00:21:22.480
free speech and so on and so forth, and you
can predict with a high degree of certainty

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:26.370
that if you come to class, then what’s going
to happen in the class - the worst that's

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:31.639
going to happen in the class, likely, is that
you're either going to get bored or hot.

00:21:31.639 --> 00:21:34.370
So that’s not so bad you know.

00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:40.009
Alright so you're going from point A to point
B, and you see your tools.

00:21:40.009 --> 00:21:43.070
I still haven't remembered that damn word.

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:47.980
What the hell is that damn word?

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:50.440
That’s so annoying.

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:51.440
Nope.

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:52.440
Whatever.

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:53.730
You go along and you see these tools.

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:59.799
These are things that are going to help get
you to your goal, and whenever you see those,

00:21:59.799 --> 00:22:05.370
you get a burst of positive emotion and basically
that means two things, that positive emotion.

00:22:05.370 --> 00:22:10.529
Think about it as a little dopamine kick,
and this is where this story differentiates

00:22:10.529 --> 00:22:12.380
a little bit from classic behaviorism.

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:16.259
So the behaviorists would say you get a little
dopamine kick and that increases the probability

00:22:16.259 --> 00:22:22.441
that the action that you just took will be
taken again in the future, and so it’s partly

00:22:22.441 --> 00:22:31.139
because the dopamine kick has a positive emotional
- it provides a positive emotional experience

00:22:31.139 --> 00:22:36.549
but it’s also because the dopamine facilitates
neural growth, and so if you're beetling along

00:22:36.549 --> 00:22:41.200
and something good happens to you, then your
brain makes the things you were doing - the

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:44.370
circuits that underlie the things you were
doing just before the good thing happened

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.730
- a little bit stronger.

00:22:46.730 --> 00:22:54.149
But, the other thing that happens when you're
beetling along and doing your positive thing

00:22:54.149 --> 00:22:58.710
is that you get a little dopamine - is that
the fact that it’s working also validates

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:04.710
your frame of reference because you know you
know might say well, if what you're doing

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:09.850
is lurking and you're getting what you want,
how is it that you're right?

00:23:09.850 --> 00:23:16.289
And the answer to that is well in some ways,
you're right enough up the entire hierarchy

00:23:16.289 --> 00:23:22.639
and so what that means is that every time
you proceed along the path that you've chosen

00:23:22.639 --> 00:23:27.870
and that's working, that entire structure
gets stabilized a bit more, and that's how

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:29.139
it should be.

00:23:29.139 --> 00:23:35.440
Now, I've tried to work out how exactly this
happens and I can give you the best example

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:36.790
by telling you about cocaine addicts.

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:44.399
So, addicts 
use to have this phrase.

00:23:44.399 --> 00:23:46.389
“Monkey on my back.”

00:23:46.389 --> 00:23:55.340
And that phrase referred to the compulsion,
the compulsive sub personality that develops

00:23:55.340 --> 00:23:58.820
that's interested in nothing but more of the
substance.

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:03.690
Now, you know, you think about that as being
hooked and you might think about it as a motivational

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:07.100
drive or a biological drive, but the thing
is it’s not like that.

00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:10.860
We’re just not that deterministic.

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:16.169
An addicted person has grown an addicted sub
personality.

00:24:16.169 --> 00:24:17.370
And I mean grown.

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:19.450
It’s not psychological.

00:24:19.450 --> 00:24:22.490
It’s psychophysiological.

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:24.049
So imagine this.

00:24:24.049 --> 00:24:26.009
Imagine this is how it works.

00:24:26.009 --> 00:24:30.419
I’ll first tell you what happens to cocaine
addicts if you take them out into a treatment

00:24:30.419 --> 00:24:31.419
center.

00:24:31.419 --> 00:24:34.740
Heroin addicts is the same thing, roughly
speaking.

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:41.340
So you take this person who’s dependant
out to a treatment center, and with heroin,

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:44.460
say, you have to let them go through withdrawal.

00:24:44.460 --> 00:24:50.620
And that takes about forty eight hours, something
like that, and you've seen movie representations

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:56.899
of it, but it’s been compared by reasonable
observers to having a very bad flu.

00:24:56.899 --> 00:25:01.570
So, alcohol withdrawal, by the way - that
kills you often.

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:03.179
You have a seizure and you die.

00:25:03.179 --> 00:25:07.009
So if you're an alcoholic and you go to the
hospital and you need to detoxify, they'll

00:25:07.009 --> 00:25:12.130
give you valium or something like that so
you don't seize yourself to death while you're

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:13.130
withdrawing.

00:25:13.130 --> 00:25:16.720
You know, when you have a hangover, part of
that is that - part of what’s happened is

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:18.900
you've actually lowered your seizure threshold.

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:22.409
Something to think about if you're prone to
seizures.

00:25:22.409 --> 00:25:25.059
It’s a bad idea to have a hangover.

00:25:25.059 --> 00:25:27.460
In fact they bred mice to study hangovers.

00:25:27.460 --> 00:25:31.789
They bred mice that could drink alcohol and
then they’d get a very bad hangover, and

00:25:31.789 --> 00:25:34.070
if you showed them a cat, they would have
a seizure.

00:25:34.070 --> 00:25:37.100
So, because the fear was enough to trigger
seizures.

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:45.039
So yeah, well you gotta get seizures going
somehow if you're gonna study them, and that's

00:25:45.039 --> 00:25:47.350
- and that can be bred.

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:53.950
So anyways, you take your heroin addict, let’s
say, and they get over their withdrawal and

00:25:53.950 --> 00:25:58.960
so that means that technically they're no
longer physiologically dependant on the drug.

00:25:58.960 --> 00:26:04.830
Now that doesn't mean their work is over by
no means.

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:09.160
But frequently the treatment center, as long
as the person is nowhere near where they usually

00:26:09.160 --> 00:26:15.679
are, once they’ve gone through withdrawal,
they can stay away from the drug quite effectively,

00:26:15.679 --> 00:26:22.620
but the problem is as soon as you put them
back in the situation that they came from,

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:26.340
and they're around the things that have been
associated with heroin, let's say, they get

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:28.779
a craving.

00:26:28.779 --> 00:26:30.870
And the cravings haven't gone away.

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:36.270
That's a different system than the system
that went through withdrawal, and so they

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:39.470
always relapse.

00:26:39.470 --> 00:26:47.440
Now, you think about how this craving works,
and I've seen this probably most seriously

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:51.749
I guess with cocaine addicts, but also with
alcoholics.

00:26:51.749 --> 00:26:56.960
So, imagine what you have to do in order to
become an addict.

00:26:56.960 --> 00:27:01.030
You know, the first thing is that you have
to start using the drug.

00:27:01.030 --> 00:27:04.659
Okay, but that - and fair enough.

00:27:04.659 --> 00:27:09.580
People experiment with all sorts of drugs
and so, you know, that's part of human nature.

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:12.789
You know, it’s risky and dangerous but it’s
not yet catastrophic.

00:27:12.789 --> 00:27:17.299
But then let’s say you start using it enough
so that you can't not use it.

00:27:17.299 --> 00:27:21.529
You have to use it everyday or something like
that, and then the next thing that happens

00:27:21.529 --> 00:27:26.259
is that it gets a little bit expensive, and
so then your life is starting to go downhill

00:27:26.259 --> 00:27:29.139
because you're spending all your time trying
to gather resources to get some more of the

00:27:29.139 --> 00:27:30.269
drug.

00:27:30.269 --> 00:27:36.600
Now, what you might ask yourself is what do
you have - like exactly what’s the chain

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:40.400
of behaviors that's being reinforced by the
drug?

00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:47.341
Well somewhere along the way, you know, maybe
when you're just starting to misuse it, there's

00:27:47.341 --> 00:27:55.369
part of you that's saying, “You know, really
this is probably not a good idea.”

00:27:55.369 --> 00:27:58.779
And then there's another part of you that
has a different thought which might be, “Oh

00:27:58.779 --> 00:27:59.789
to hell with it.

00:27:59.789 --> 00:28:01.259
It’s only one.

00:28:01.259 --> 00:28:02.730
It’s only tonight.”

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:07.679
And so then you take the drug, and then there's
chain leading from the thought to the drug

00:28:07.679 --> 00:28:10.289
behavior.

00:28:10.289 --> 00:28:13.879
But the drug reinforces all the way back along
that chain.

00:28:13.879 --> 00:28:17.679
So what happens is that little system that
thinks, “Oh to hell with it, it’s only

00:28:17.679 --> 00:28:20.999
one night,” gets a little stronger.

00:28:20.999 --> 00:28:27.600
Now, it doesn't get as strong as the systems
that are being reinforced that are activated

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:36.730
directly before the drug hit, but the thing
is that maybe you take heroin in fifty different

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:40.759
situations and so you're conditioning yourself
to cues in all those different situations.

00:28:40.759 --> 00:28:44.980
You use slightly different behaviors and so
on, so there's different things being shaped,

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:49.580
but one of the things that's always being
reinforced, even though not that much, is

00:28:49.580 --> 00:28:55.009
this thought pattern, and the thought pattern
is something like “Oh to hell with it, doesn’t

00:28:55.009 --> 00:28:59.019
matter anyways” because you have to think
that in order to - especially at the beginning

00:28:59.019 --> 00:29:01.159
of a nice downhill trip.

00:29:01.159 --> 00:29:05.450
You have to think that, and so what happens
is you build this monster in your head.

00:29:05.450 --> 00:29:11.779
It’s full of lies and rationalizations,
and they are whatever lies and rationalizations

00:29:11.779 --> 00:29:15.929
allowed you to continue the behavior to begin
with.

00:29:15.929 --> 00:29:20.720
But you're making them more and more potent,
and so you build a sub personality inside

00:29:20.720 --> 00:29:25.640
of you that it’s a one eyed monster, fundamentally.

00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:32.020
All it wants is the drug, and it’s armed
with any number of perfectly, depends on how

00:29:32.020 --> 00:29:33.580
smart you are, you know.

00:29:33.580 --> 00:29:41.730
You can come up with a very powerful, nihilistic
rational argument why - about why life is

00:29:41.730 --> 00:29:46.679
futile and your future is dead and there's
nothing better to do anyways, and you'd be

00:29:46.679 --> 00:29:50.860
a fool to engage in such a destructive society,
and etcetera, etcetera.

00:29:50.860 --> 00:29:56.470
Like you can conjure up a very - an argument
that’s virtually impermeable to rational

00:29:56.470 --> 00:29:59.350
argument.

00:29:59.350 --> 00:30:03.080
Rational counterargument.

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:05.860
Because you know, there's some things that
you just can't prove.

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:14.330
Like you can't prove life is intrinsically
worthwhile you know, because for obvious reasons.

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:20.110
So, now the reason I told you that is because
I think it’s a good example of how things

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:27.049
are nested inside of each other you know,
and also the relationship between this very

00:30:27.049 --> 00:30:31.700
large nesting of concentric lenses, let’s
say, and the way that those things are shaped

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:34.049
and reinforced across time.

00:30:34.049 --> 00:30:38.950
Whenever you're acting locally, and something
goes the way you want it to, let’s say,

00:30:38.950 --> 00:30:42.749
and not the way you expect because that's
what Sokolov, and Vinogradova, and Jeffrey

00:30:42.749 --> 00:30:46.600
Gray, and the people who were looking at the
orienting response first presumed.

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:51.919
They’d presumed that you wanted things to
go the way you expect, but that's not exactly

00:30:51.919 --> 00:30:54.620
right, and it’s an important distinction.

00:30:54.620 --> 00:30:59.539
You want things to go the way you want them
to go, and if it’s just expectation then

00:30:59.539 --> 00:31:02.519
it’s just a cold, cognitive model of the
world right.

00:31:02.519 --> 00:31:03.980
It’s like robotic.

00:31:03.980 --> 00:31:06.059
Okay, A happened, now B should happen.

00:31:06.059 --> 00:31:07.059
Now C should happen.

00:31:07.059 --> 00:31:09.159
It’s like that isn't how you think at all.

00:31:09.159 --> 00:31:13.580
You're always inside a motivational frame,
and it isn't only that you expect the next

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:19.200
thing to happen, but if the next thing happens,
it matters whether it’s what you want or

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:20.200
not.

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:24.239
If it’s what you want, then that little
system is reinforced.

00:31:24.239 --> 00:31:29.950
It’s pleasurable for you, and then the whole
hierarchy grows a little bit stronger, and

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:34.539
if it is not what you want then you have a
problem.

00:31:34.539 --> 00:31:37.270
And that's where things get weird and interesting.

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:43.749
So okay, so you're going along from point
A to point B, and you can see tools and you

00:31:43.749 --> 00:31:47.919
can see obstacles, and obstacles are anything
that get in your path.

00:31:47.919 --> 00:31:51.840
What that means in some sense is that what
a tool is and what an obstacle is switches

00:31:51.840 --> 00:31:55.850
very rapidly as you change focus, and as your
intent changes.

00:31:55.850 --> 00:31:57.889
So it’s a dynamic system.

00:31:57.889 --> 00:32:03.619
Alright, and so the way emotion works in this
regard is if things are going the way you

00:32:03.619 --> 00:32:08.600
want them to then you feel positive emotion
and that keeps you moving towards your goal,

00:32:08.600 --> 00:32:12.340
and if you run into an obstacle, then you
feel negative emotion.

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:19.980
Okay so, the next issue might be well just
exactly what should you experience when you

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:21.179
experience negative emotion?

00:32:21.179 --> 00:32:30.440
Well you might say well that's a tricky question
because if you encounter an obstacle, that

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:35.119
means that something about your model is wrong
because if it was right, you wouldn't have

00:32:35.119 --> 00:32:36.119
encountered that obstacle.

00:32:36.119 --> 00:32:38.879
So this is a very interesting way of thinking.

00:32:38.879 --> 00:32:44.519
It’s a pragmatic way of thinking because
what it illustrates is that whenever you do

00:32:44.519 --> 00:32:52.389
something, you're acting out a theory of truth,
and the theory of truth is the actions that

00:32:52.389 --> 00:32:57.389
I’m undertaking at the moment are sufficient
to reach the goal that I’m aiming at, and

00:32:57.389 --> 00:33:02.389
so you set up a theory of truth with each
act.

00:33:02.389 --> 00:33:09.850
So, you're always struck with the reality
of truth and error or correct and incorrect

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:17.249
because you can't posit that a goal is worth
having, and if there's a path to it that might

00:33:17.249 --> 00:33:23.660
be clear or obstructed without also saying
it matters whether you know what you're doing.

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:31.299
Okay, so you hit an obstacle, and that produces
negative emotion.

00:33:31.299 --> 00:33:38.889
Now, the problem you have here is that in
principle, the reason you hit that obstacle

00:33:38.889 --> 00:33:44.820
could be that there's an error in your nested
model at any level of the model.

00:33:44.820 --> 00:33:47.620
So and that's why I told you about the little
story of the sun.

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:52.580
It’s like normally you just turn your computer
on and off because something has happened

00:33:52.580 --> 00:33:57.549
that’s local, but now and then your problem
is that the sun isn't as reliable as you might

00:33:57.549 --> 00:33:59.159
like it to be.

00:33:59.159 --> 00:34:05.230
And so, you're faced with this insanely complex
problem not when things go right because when

00:34:05.230 --> 00:34:09.190
things go right, you can just continue ignoring
everything that you’re ignoring, but when

00:34:09.190 --> 00:34:15.380
they go wrong, the search space that opens
up before you is just about infinite.

00:34:15.380 --> 00:34:18.659
Now, that has real world consequences.

00:34:18.659 --> 00:34:23.250
I mean, one of the things that happens when
people develop panic disorder - what seems

00:34:23.250 --> 00:34:24.820
to happen is that…

00:34:24.820 --> 00:34:29.859
I can tell you the typical story about how
you would develop panic disorder with agoraphobia

00:34:29.859 --> 00:34:31.470
cause the two things are somewhat separate.

00:34:31.470 --> 00:34:35.520
So generally this happens to - it generally
happens to women.

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:37.490
It generally happens in their forties.

00:34:37.490 --> 00:34:43.250
Women are more susceptible to anxiety and
depression, and men are more susceptible to

00:34:43.250 --> 00:34:50.419
substance abuse and antisocial behavior.

00:34:50.419 --> 00:34:52.730
Figures that something would go wrong right
now doesn't it?

00:34:52.730 --> 00:34:55.929
Okay, so now what do I ask myself?

00:34:55.929 --> 00:34:57.530
Well, is this relevant.

00:34:57.530 --> 00:34:59.190
Is it going to disrupt the class?

00:34:59.190 --> 00:35:03.059
Does it mean that there's something wrong
with my computer?

00:35:03.059 --> 00:35:05.660
Should I call the help people?

00:35:05.660 --> 00:35:12.500
And I’ll say no we’ll ignore it on the
assumption it will continue working, and until

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:15.329
that hypothesis fails then we can ignore it.

00:35:15.329 --> 00:35:18.829
Okay so…

00:35:18.829 --> 00:35:22.020
So something goes wrong and this search space
opens up in front of you.

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:23.910
Now okay, back to the agoraphobic.

00:35:23.910 --> 00:35:29.799
So what happens to her usually is generally
these are people who’ve had dependant relationships

00:35:29.799 --> 00:35:37.880
and perhaps they had a relatively overprotective
family, or let’s just say protective family,

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:43.559
and then they had an intimate relationship
early in their life and maybe that lasted

00:35:43.559 --> 00:35:50.990
quite a long time or maybe they're just married
to the same guy, but the - and inside that

00:35:50.990 --> 00:35:55.150
relationship, she’s relatively dependant
which means probably she doesn't go anywhere

00:35:55.150 --> 00:36:00.560
without her husband, you know, she doesn't
do a lot of things autonomously let’s say.

00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:10.829
Okay, so then she hits menopause and you know,
she’s getting some strange physiological

00:36:10.829 --> 00:36:14.520
symptoms from that of various sorts.

00:36:14.520 --> 00:36:20.300
And maybe at the same time, maybe she talks
to her husband about a divorce or maybe he

00:36:20.300 --> 00:36:25.160
talks to her about a divorce or maybe her
sister gets divorced or maybe a close friend

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:33.040
dies or maybe she starts to experience heart
palpitations because of the menopause.

00:36:33.040 --> 00:36:37.730
And then she thinks, “Oh my god.

00:36:37.730 --> 00:36:39.920
That could happen to me.

00:36:39.920 --> 00:36:42.390
It’s like my whole world can fall apart.”

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:43.630
So that’s one fear.

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:45.730
“My whole world could fall apart.”

00:36:45.730 --> 00:36:50.369
So that's sort of the fear of societal catastrophe
- the collapse of the structure.

00:36:50.369 --> 00:36:53.309
And then the other fear is often, “Oh my
god.

00:36:53.309 --> 00:36:54.720
I could be dying of a heart attack.”

00:36:54.720 --> 00:36:59.619
And so what happens to people who start to
develop panic disorder is that they end up

00:36:59.619 --> 00:37:04.859
going to the emergency room all the time to
be checked out by cardiologists, and the cardiologists

00:37:04.859 --> 00:37:08.730
are used to this, and they generally check
people up quite extensively but most of the

00:37:08.730 --> 00:37:12.220
time if there's something up with your heart,
there's nothing wrong.

00:37:12.220 --> 00:37:23.960
But there could be, and so then the sort of…
what happens is the person who is developed

00:37:23.960 --> 00:37:29.730
agoraphobia starts thinking perhaps about
what would happen if her husband died or if

00:37:29.730 --> 00:37:33.569
they got divorced, and then she starts thinking
about the fact that well she doesn't really

00:37:33.569 --> 00:37:37.130
know what she’d do, and she doesn't really
know how to take care of a bunch of things

00:37:37.130 --> 00:37:40.700
and how she might take care of the finances
and so forth.

00:37:40.700 --> 00:37:49.270
And so, she has no real place to turn, and
so then she makes the fatal mistake.

00:37:49.270 --> 00:37:53.200
She goes out and she starts to panic, and
then she goes home.

00:37:53.200 --> 00:37:54.470
That's not good.

00:37:54.470 --> 00:38:00.720
So if you ever develop severe anxiety, the
one thing you don't want to do is avoid because

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:05.291
of it because as soon as you avoid because
of it, it gets worse, and the reason for that

00:38:05.291 --> 00:38:10.750
is imagine her anxiety systems are trying
to figure out how afraid she should be, and

00:38:10.750 --> 00:38:14.480
it ranges from panic stricken to none.

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:20.950
Well, the anxiety systems have all sorts of
sources of input because they're trying to

00:38:20.950 --> 00:38:26.420
calculate a very difficult problem because,
you know, when this thing flickered, one of

00:38:26.420 --> 00:38:31.640
the things that everyone does is look around,
and the reason you do that - it’s called

00:38:31.640 --> 00:38:32.640
referencing.

00:38:32.640 --> 00:38:33.640
Social referencing.

00:38:33.640 --> 00:38:35.050
It’s like, “Well how upset should I be
about this?

00:38:35.050 --> 00:38:38.839
What’s going on?” and you look at everyone’s
face and you see well they're upset or they're

00:38:38.839 --> 00:38:42.620
not upset and everybody kind of comes to an
average agreement about how upsetting this

00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:48.650
is, and we do that very, very rapidly, you
know, and we estimate how anxious we should

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:56.420
be by our default neuroticism, so I would
say generally speaking, if you have higher

00:38:56.420 --> 00:39:02.809
levels of negative emotion, rather than lower
levels, what happens is you - the severity

00:39:02.809 --> 00:39:10.569
that you estimate a given uncertain event
to exist to have is higher than the severity

00:39:10.569 --> 00:39:14.630
someone else might estimate if they were more
emotionally stable.

00:39:14.630 --> 00:39:19.610
And then there's another input which is well
roughly how competent do people around you

00:39:19.610 --> 00:39:24.760
and you think you are because, you know, how
afraid you should be is actually somewhat

00:39:24.760 --> 00:39:27.180
proportionate to how competent you are right?

00:39:27.180 --> 00:39:32.760
I mean, if you're a well practiced hunter
and you're out in the forest, and a bear charges

00:39:32.760 --> 00:39:35.809
you, it’s like well maybe you’ve seen
a hundred bears and you know what to do and

00:39:35.809 --> 00:39:40.750
it’s like it’s a bear and all that, but
you're not gonna run.

00:39:40.750 --> 00:39:45.030
And so then the bear probably won't think
you're prey because the bear thinks that if

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:46.910
you run, that you're edible.

00:39:46.910 --> 00:39:52.381
Now the thing that's really interesting is
that if you watch yourself run, then your

00:39:52.381 --> 00:39:54.640
brain thinks you're edible too.

00:39:54.640 --> 00:40:00.010
So that's another input so because - so the
anxiety system is doing this terribly difficult

00:40:00.010 --> 00:40:02.190
calibration job which is “Okay something
happened.

00:40:02.190 --> 00:40:03.589
How upset should I be?”

00:40:03.589 --> 00:40:05.930
Well, how upset are other people?

00:40:05.930 --> 00:40:08.020
What’s my physiological response?

00:40:08.020 --> 00:40:10.230
How does it compare to other times I was upset.

00:40:10.230 --> 00:40:11.230
What are my options?

00:40:11.230 --> 00:40:15.220
That's a good one because one of the things
the anxiety systems really wants is that it

00:40:15.220 --> 00:40:17.210
wants you to come up with a plan.

00:40:17.210 --> 00:40:21.550
It doesn't really care what the plan is although
you have to believe it because it’s not

00:40:21.550 --> 00:40:26.200
really a sophisticated cognitive system, it’s
an alarm bell and it says, “Look, something’s

00:40:26.200 --> 00:40:27.200
up here.

00:40:27.200 --> 00:40:30.619
You have to knit up your world again, and
put it to rest.

00:40:30.619 --> 00:40:34.329
We need to be able to ignore everything again,”
and if you can't do that, that thing will

00:40:34.329 --> 00:40:35.410
just go off forever.

00:40:35.410 --> 00:40:39.260
That’s what happens at least in part to
people who have post traumatic stress disorder.

00:40:39.260 --> 00:40:44.190
They're soldiers, you know, and maybe they've
gone out in battle and they've watched themself

00:40:44.190 --> 00:40:48.880
do terrible things cause that's actually one
of the most common precedents of post traumatic

00:40:48.880 --> 00:40:56.140
stress disorder, and they have no idea how
to represent that in their hierarchical world

00:40:56.140 --> 00:41:00.030
model, you know, because part of your world
model, a huge part of it - the “What is”

00:41:00.030 --> 00:41:02.280
part, you know, is who you are.

00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:06.119
Well it’s like you might find out that you're
not who you think you are.

00:41:06.119 --> 00:41:09.099
You aren't even who you think you are a little
tiny bit.

00:41:09.099 --> 00:41:15.359
Like there's corners and nooks and whole unexplored
rooms inside of you that contain things that

00:41:15.359 --> 00:41:19.570
if they ever come out, you're done.

00:41:19.570 --> 00:41:21.000
You don't know what to do with it, you know.

00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:25.150
And if you've dealt with something who has
post traumatic stress disorder, it’s very

00:41:25.150 --> 00:41:30.160
frequently the case you have to - they have
to learn a philosophy of good and evil in

00:41:30.160 --> 00:41:31.170
order to recover.

00:41:31.170 --> 00:41:35.089
It has to be sophisticated because they've
seen these terrible, destructive forces at

00:41:35.089 --> 00:41:42.900
work and their whole world model is just not
handling it.

00:41:42.900 --> 00:41:49.569
So what happens is that the agoraphobic person
runs, and then their anxiety system says well

00:41:49.569 --> 00:41:55.339
what's chasing them is obviously a flesh eating
predator, and you know it isn't exactly that

00:41:55.339 --> 00:41:57.700
it thinks that although it might have dreams
like that.

00:41:57.700 --> 00:42:04.500
They could easily have dreams of being pursued,
but it’s the same system like the system

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:09.200
that we use to run away from things that ate
us when we were thirty pounds is the same

00:42:09.200 --> 00:42:13.940
system that we now use to process relatively
abstract threats.

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:15.289
And why wouldn't it be?

00:42:15.289 --> 00:42:18.420
Look, as far as I’m concerned, this isn't
even a hypothesis.

00:42:18.420 --> 00:42:21.369
It’s like we know evolution is conservative.

00:42:21.369 --> 00:42:23.670
Everything builds on what was there before.

00:42:23.670 --> 00:42:28.789
So, what else are you gonna use when you're
threatened by some abstract threat except

00:42:28.789 --> 00:42:33.170
the system that you used to use when you were
threatened by a crocodile?

00:42:33.170 --> 00:42:35.839
So, it’s only…

00:42:35.839 --> 00:42:41.190
it’s exactly the way it works.

00:42:41.190 --> 00:42:47.339
So then you know, she ends up - question?

00:42:47.339 --> 00:42:59.490
Oh no, no you have to run from something.

00:42:59.490 --> 00:43:00.839
Just running, that’s irrelevant.

00:43:00.839 --> 00:43:02.640
It’s a huge difference.

00:43:02.640 --> 00:43:05.930
If you're running towards something, that’s
way different than if you're running away

00:43:05.930 --> 00:43:06.930
from something.

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:08.530
Say you’re like anxious about an assignment
and you go for a run and come back more clear

00:43:08.530 --> 00:43:10.819
headed to work on it.

00:43:10.819 --> 00:43:13.030
You're not running from the assignment.

00:43:13.030 --> 00:43:14.171
Are you not?

00:43:14.171 --> 00:43:16.819
No, you're just going out for a run, and that's
okay.

00:43:16.819 --> 00:43:18.059
Look, let me give you an example.

00:43:18.059 --> 00:43:19.059
Well it’s a good question.

00:43:19.059 --> 00:43:21.420
Let me give you an example.

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:28.470
If you take a stressor of a certain magnitude
and you are interested in the response of

00:43:28.470 --> 00:43:35.680
two groups, if you let one group handle the
stressor voluntarily, and you force the other

00:43:35.680 --> 00:43:40.930
group to handle the stressor involuntarily,
the second group will produce much more cortisol

00:43:40.930 --> 00:43:48.950
which is the primary stress hormone, and so
voluntary approach - you use different psychophysiological

00:43:48.950 --> 00:43:53.400
systems for voluntary approach than you do
for retreat and you know, part of what you're

00:43:53.400 --> 00:43:57.540
doing when you're training someone or when
you're using exposure with someone who has

00:43:57.540 --> 00:44:05.079
agoraphobia is that you're teaching them that
the voluntary approach system, if you activate

00:44:05.079 --> 00:44:07.500
that, that’s a very good way of coping with
anxiety.

00:44:07.500 --> 00:44:11.020
Now they don't know that, in this situation
that we’re describing, because they're not

00:44:11.020 --> 00:44:12.020
autonomous.

00:44:12.020 --> 00:44:15.160
They haven't ever learned to rely on that
system.

00:44:15.160 --> 00:44:20.950
So you know, if you're faced with a threat,
there's a variety of things you can do.

00:44:20.950 --> 00:44:25.320
You can just panic, but panic is usually distress,
you know.

00:44:25.320 --> 00:44:27.500
Maybe a child sort of does that when it starts
crying.

00:44:27.500 --> 00:44:29.760
And what happens when the child starts crying?

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:32.109
Well, someone comes to its rescue right.

00:44:32.109 --> 00:44:36.910
So one of the things you can do is emit distress
cries and social resources will come and be

00:44:36.910 --> 00:44:39.770
gathered around you and maybe that'll solve
the problem.

00:44:39.770 --> 00:44:45.609
But that doesn't work when your heart is palpitating
because who’s gonna fix that?

00:44:45.609 --> 00:44:47.040
Well often these people...

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:53.131
If you look deeply into their thoughts maybe
they're afraid to ride a subway and the actual

00:44:53.131 --> 00:44:56.730
reason they're afraid to ride the subway is
they think, “Well what if the subway gets

00:44:56.730 --> 00:45:02.950
stopped between stations and I’m trapped
on here and I have a heart attack and I can't

00:45:02.950 --> 00:45:04.599
get to the hospital?”

00:45:04.599 --> 00:45:09.960
So that hospital thing is running around in
their head all the time because their default

00:45:09.960 --> 00:45:14.720
strategy for dealing with threat is to seek
authority, and one of the things that often

00:45:14.720 --> 00:45:20.599
sets people up for developing agoraphobia
is that the authority that they rely on suddenly

00:45:20.599 --> 00:45:22.800
becomes uncertain for one reason or another.

00:45:22.800 --> 00:45:27.530
Maybe the person is going to leave and they're
isolated or maybe the person, like if it’s

00:45:27.530 --> 00:45:30.070
a husband say, the person is - what good is
that?

00:45:30.070 --> 00:45:32.250
If your heart’s palpitating and you're gonna
have a heart attack?

00:45:32.250 --> 00:45:35.900
It’s a problem that can't be solved that
way.

00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:40.100
You know, people might think about that as
a whole existential crisis, and it is an existential

00:45:40.100 --> 00:45:41.100
crisis.

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:44.540
It’s like you're coming face to face with
your own mortality, and that's certainly the

00:45:44.540 --> 00:45:50.280
case if you're going through menopause at
the same time because that's another, you

00:45:50.280 --> 00:45:55.900
know, profound life shift that indicates you're
sort of halfway done, or two-thirds of the

00:45:55.900 --> 00:45:57.060
way done.

00:45:57.060 --> 00:45:58.060
Something like that.

00:45:58.060 --> 00:46:01.819
And that's of course, you know, you can think
about that in terms of terror management as

00:46:01.819 --> 00:46:02.819
well.

00:46:02.819 --> 00:46:06.010
So it is an existential crisis, and the question
is how do you deal with it?

00:46:06.010 --> 00:46:12.070
And the answer to that generally is by becoming
a lot tougher than you are, and the way you

00:46:12.070 --> 00:46:18.700
help people do that is teach them there are
certain dangerous situations they can approach

00:46:18.700 --> 00:46:20.430
and they can approach them.

00:46:20.430 --> 00:46:27.660
And generally, people learn this quite quickly,
you know, and so the psychoanalysts believe

00:46:27.660 --> 00:46:33.039
that if you just - this was their early criticisms
of behavioral exposure was that, you know,

00:46:33.039 --> 00:46:39.180
if you expose someone who’s afraid, say,
just to an elevator that that will help their

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:44.390
other fears or maybe the fear will be substituted
somewhere else because it’s just symptomatic.

00:46:44.390 --> 00:46:45.530
But that didn't work.

00:46:45.530 --> 00:46:51.171
It’s not right, and it’s because when
you expose someone to an elevator, you're

00:46:51.171 --> 00:46:52.820
not exposing them to an elevator.

00:46:52.820 --> 00:46:57.460
If they're afraid of that elevator, you're
exposing them to their own death.

00:46:57.460 --> 00:47:03.820
So the elevator, you could say well the reason
behavioral exposure works - and it generalizes

00:47:03.820 --> 00:47:09.660
- is because it’s not easy to specify what
the stimulus is.

00:47:09.660 --> 00:47:12.960
I mean, when I’m in front of an elevator,
it’s an elevator.

00:47:12.960 --> 00:47:18.150
When someone with agoraphobia is in front
of an elevator, it’s not an elevator.

00:47:18.150 --> 00:47:19.420
That’s the problem.

00:47:19.420 --> 00:47:22.440
If it was just an elevator, they’d just
get on it and away they go.

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:26.160
So you know, I had one client who was afraid
of elevators and the elevator opened up and

00:47:26.160 --> 00:47:28.440
she said, “That’s a tomb.”

00:47:28.440 --> 00:47:32.650
And it was just an immediate response, you
know, but you could say well that was the

00:47:32.650 --> 00:47:39.099
symbolic representation that came to mind,
but you could also say that well there are

00:47:39.099 --> 00:47:42.799
similarities between an elevator and a tomb,
and you can get trapped inside an elevator

00:47:42.799 --> 00:47:47.410
and you could get trapped inside an elevator
and die in which case it would be a tomb,

00:47:47.410 --> 00:47:51.490
and the logical conclusion from that is that
everything could be a tomb.

00:47:51.490 --> 00:47:54.480
So you might think, “Well the stimulus isn’t
tomb.”

00:47:54.480 --> 00:47:55.700
Like yeah, yeah.

00:47:55.700 --> 00:47:56.700
Wrong.

00:47:56.700 --> 00:47:57.730
It can be tomb.

00:47:57.730 --> 00:48:04.890
It’s unlikely, but then you might also ask
yourself this.

00:48:04.890 --> 00:48:09.650
If the chance that you're going to die somewhere
is a hundred thousand to one, should you be

00:48:09.650 --> 00:48:10.650
upset?

00:48:10.650 --> 00:48:16.819
And well the answer to that is dying is of
infinite magnitude, and if you multiply one

00:48:16.819 --> 00:48:21.680
and a hundred thousand by infinite magnitude,
you get a very very large number.

00:48:21.680 --> 00:48:25.410
And so one of the things that makes you wonder
is why the hell are people terrified out of

00:48:25.410 --> 00:48:27.380
their skin all the time?

00:48:27.380 --> 00:48:32.900
That is a very good question because by no
means obvious that people aren’t, and I

00:48:32.900 --> 00:48:37.840
actually think - I often think that the mystery
with a condition like agoraphobia isn't that

00:48:37.840 --> 00:48:39.119
some people have agoraphobia.

00:48:39.119 --> 00:48:43.200
It’s that everyone doesn't have it all the
time.

00:48:43.200 --> 00:48:47.400
So because - and you know the terror management
people talk about this too is that because

00:48:47.400 --> 00:48:52.789
we’re always confronting our own mortality
and a world of infinite complexity, why aren't

00:48:52.789 --> 00:48:55.400
we constantly in a state of panic?

00:48:55.400 --> 00:48:56.860
And you know, now and then you realize that.

00:48:56.860 --> 00:48:59.140
You think jeez, why aren't we in a state of
panic?

00:48:59.140 --> 00:49:09.880
It’s a very unsettling realization, and
why it isn't more common, that’s a very,

00:49:09.880 --> 00:49:15.390
very complicated question.

00:49:15.390 --> 00:49:21.680
I think a lot of it is that we imitate other
people, and so a lot of our faith, let’s

00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:27.270
say, in the stability of our lives and the
utility and the meaning of our lives is actually

00:49:27.270 --> 00:49:32.210
instantiated at an embodied level where it’s
not subject to criticism.

00:49:32.210 --> 00:49:36.070
So for example, I’ll give you an example.

00:49:36.070 --> 00:49:42.080
There's a child two years old and there's
a mother, and they're together in the kitchen,

00:49:42.080 --> 00:49:44.740
and a mouse runs across the floor.

00:49:44.740 --> 00:49:49.790
Okay, the child orients towards the mouse,
watches it zip across, and then looks at his

00:49:49.790 --> 00:49:51.529
mother or her mother.

00:49:51.529 --> 00:49:52.529
Why?

00:49:52.529 --> 00:50:00.021
Well because something anomalous has occurred,
the significance of which is unstated, and

00:50:00.021 --> 00:50:01.021
it’s arousing.

00:50:01.021 --> 00:50:04.720
It’s like wow look at that, you know, you
can be startled like that like “Wow look

00:50:04.720 --> 00:50:05.720
at that!”

00:50:05.720 --> 00:50:06.720
Zip!

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:09.060
Well then you think, what does mum think of
that?

00:50:09.060 --> 00:50:11.921
And mum is thinking, “Oh that’s a mouse,”
maybe she’ll tell you about mice or maybe

00:50:11.921 --> 00:50:14.420
she’ll set a trap or something.

00:50:14.420 --> 00:50:19.869
But she’s calm, and so the child imitates
that, and then you know, they've got this

00:50:19.869 --> 00:50:24.579
sense in their body that human beings can
deal with mice and that’s no problem.

00:50:24.579 --> 00:50:28.299
The alternative is that the mouse comes up
and the mother jumps up on the table and starts

00:50:28.299 --> 00:50:29.299
screaming.

00:50:29.299 --> 00:50:33.720
It’s like well, you could be sure what’s
the child gonna do in a situation like that

00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:37.490
is burst into tears and be extraordinarily
upset.

00:50:37.490 --> 00:50:42.670
So a lot of what we’re doing when we’re
little, and we watch people around us is that

00:50:42.670 --> 00:50:44.700
they model being calm.

00:50:44.700 --> 00:50:51.779
So we just adopt the implicit assumption - it’s
an embodied assumption - that you can be calm

00:50:51.779 --> 00:50:56.789
in these circumstances and luckily enough,
we generally don't - that generally doesn't

00:50:56.789 --> 00:51:02.270
come unglued, but you can unglue it even by
deep thought, and that happens to people quite

00:51:02.270 --> 00:51:03.270
frequently.

00:51:03.270 --> 00:51:04.799
That would be an existential catastrophe.

00:51:04.799 --> 00:51:16.309
Alright so, let me show you something else
here.

00:51:16.309 --> 00:51:24.520
So, same idea.

00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:26.640
Now, this is changed a little bit.

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:30.440
So you're going from point A to point B, and
what can happen?

00:51:30.440 --> 00:51:34.809
Well you can encounter tools and you can encounter
obstacles and you can zero almost everything

00:51:34.809 --> 00:51:35.930
out.

00:51:35.930 --> 00:51:37.960
But you can look at it this way too.

00:51:37.960 --> 00:51:41.070
When you're moving forward, you can encounter
what you predict.

00:51:41.070 --> 00:51:45.520
That should really be desired rather than
predictiveness in the old diagram, or you

00:51:45.520 --> 00:51:52.480
can encounter something that you didn't predict
or desire, and if you encounter something

00:51:52.480 --> 00:51:56.359
that you predict and desire, then you have
positive emotion and we know why that is.

00:51:56.359 --> 00:51:59.549
You're getting to where you want to go and
it validates the whole structure.

00:51:59.549 --> 00:52:02.950
But then, you run into something unpredicted.

00:52:02.950 --> 00:52:09.970
Well then, you get - that’s a threat and
you get anxious.

00:52:09.970 --> 00:52:13.119
Well you also get exploratory.

00:52:13.119 --> 00:52:17.369
So then you might say well, how anxious do
you get and how exploratory do you get, and

00:52:17.369 --> 00:52:23.770
I would say it’s proportional - imagine
the hierarchy again.

00:52:23.770 --> 00:52:31.609
If you feel that it’s just an inconvenience
that you can make a mild alteration and direct

00:52:31.609 --> 00:52:37.260
action and go over, you'll be mildly inconvenienced
and maybe you’ll be a bit curious, but what

00:52:37.260 --> 00:52:43.190
happens is that as your suspicion that the
error is occurring at higher and higher and

00:52:43.190 --> 00:52:49.080
broader and broader levels in the hierarchy,
the probability that you're going to be anxious,

00:52:49.080 --> 00:52:54.570
and quite anxious compared to exploratory
at least initially, starts to mount.

00:52:54.570 --> 00:52:58.800
So, and then, all those other factors also
play a role.

00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:03.680
You know, your social status, your neuroticism,
your sense of self competence.

00:53:03.680 --> 00:53:10.190
How much you've explored all those other things
is a very complicated thing to address, and

00:53:10.190 --> 00:53:12.900
so alright.

00:53:12.900 --> 00:53:18.279
So then you're going to concentrate on what
happens if you experience an unpredicted outcome

00:53:18.279 --> 00:53:20.920
because here's the complicated question.

00:53:20.920 --> 00:53:25.890
If you experience an unpredicted outcome,
then you have a weird problem.

00:53:25.890 --> 00:53:30.809
The problem is that you have to figure out
what to do about something that you don't

00:53:30.809 --> 00:53:39.010
know what to do about, and that’s a big
problem.

00:53:39.010 --> 00:53:50.059
So...let’s start with this one.

00:53:50.059 --> 00:53:54.330
Alright so, you're going.

00:53:54.330 --> 00:53:55.760
You're in the top left hand corner.

00:53:55.760 --> 00:54:01.640
You're in this little frame, and you're going
from point A to point B, and you encounter

00:54:01.640 --> 00:54:07.590
- I’m calling it now - an anomaly, and anomaly
is something that shouldn't be according to

00:54:07.590 --> 00:54:10.570
your model of the world.

00:54:10.570 --> 00:54:16.250
Now, what that does is it destabilizes that
model at some level of resolution.

00:54:16.250 --> 00:54:23.299
Now what we’re assuming here is that this
is a relatively high order disruption.

00:54:23.299 --> 00:54:25.819
Okay so I can give you some examples of that.

00:54:25.819 --> 00:54:31.020
The heart palpitation example was a good one
cause that'll kind of unglue you.

00:54:31.020 --> 00:54:34.260
Maybe you take your child to the hospital
because they have a minor illness and you

00:54:34.260 --> 00:54:38.020
find out that they don't have a minor illness,
they have a major illness, or you find that

00:54:38.020 --> 00:54:42.750
out about yourself or you find that about
your partner, or maybe the stock market crashes

00:54:42.750 --> 00:54:47.730
or you have a car accident or you write the
MCAT and you end up in the twenty fifth percentile

00:54:47.730 --> 00:54:52.680
like the quarter of the people who take it
do, and then all of a sudden bang.

00:54:52.680 --> 00:54:55.931
You're not where you thought you were, and
you're not going to where you thought where

00:54:55.931 --> 00:55:01.910
you were going and you don't know what the
hell's going on, and that can be as we said,

00:55:01.910 --> 00:55:03.020
a small revolution.

00:55:03.020 --> 00:55:08.920
You just have to make a detour around it,
or it can just sucker punch you, and that's

00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:13.329
as far as I've been able to determine, that's
Alice down the rabbit hole.

00:55:13.329 --> 00:55:16.930
That's a descent to the underworld, and what's
down in the underworld?

00:55:16.930 --> 00:55:22.589
Well partly what’s down - everything is
down there in the underworld, and the reason

00:55:22.589 --> 00:55:29.750
for that...it’s very complicated but part
of the reason is that when things fall apart,

00:55:29.750 --> 00:55:34.951
you don't get to ignore things anymore because
you had this model that told you what was

00:55:34.951 --> 00:55:39.010
relevant in the world and what wasn't and
the real important part of that model was

00:55:39.010 --> 00:55:44.940
that it told you what wasn't relevant, and
then all of a sudden something pops up and

00:55:44.940 --> 00:55:45.940
takes you down.

00:55:45.940 --> 00:55:48.420
It’s like everything's relevant.

00:55:48.420 --> 00:55:53.500
Everything's potentially relevant, and that's
terribly stressful because everything is should

00:55:53.500 --> 00:55:54.500
I be angry?

00:55:54.500 --> 00:55:55.559
Should I be upset?

00:55:55.559 --> 00:55:56.559
Should I cry?

00:55:56.559 --> 00:55:57.559
Should I get divorced?

00:55:57.559 --> 00:55:58.559
Should I run away?

00:55:58.559 --> 00:55:59.559
Should I commit suicide?

00:55:59.559 --> 00:56:01.940
It’s like how am I come up with a new plan?

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:02.940
Where am I gonna get money?

00:56:02.940 --> 00:56:04.020
Who’s going to help me?

00:56:04.020 --> 00:56:06.299
What’s my future going to look like?

00:56:06.299 --> 00:56:07.569
What does my past look like?

00:56:07.569 --> 00:56:08.619
What am I going to do today?

00:56:08.619 --> 00:56:09.619
What am I gonna do tomorrow?

00:56:09.619 --> 00:56:14.720
It’s like all of these things come up and
some of that weirdness in the external world,

00:56:14.720 --> 00:56:19.329
and that would be well, let’s say that you
discover that your child’s sick, and let's

00:56:19.329 --> 00:56:22.190
say the doctors really don't know what to
do about it.

00:56:22.190 --> 00:56:27.890
Okay so, you've just been handed a problem
of infinite complexity, and a problem of infinite

00:56:27.890 --> 00:56:32.579
complexity is like a journey through a maze
that has no end.

00:56:32.579 --> 00:56:37.210
It’s a branching maze, and so what you'll
find yourself doing, for example, is going

00:56:37.210 --> 00:56:42.279
on the internet and spending hundreds of hours
- if you've got a research oriented mind - hundreds

00:56:42.279 --> 00:56:47.079
of hours looking up every single research
article you can possibly find on this condition,

00:56:47.079 --> 00:56:52.060
and that'll range from like the farthest out
new age ideas to the most cutting edge science,

00:56:52.060 --> 00:56:53.200
if you can handle that.

00:56:53.200 --> 00:56:55.609
So, you know, good luck with that.

00:56:55.609 --> 00:57:02.140
That’s an infinite search, and then that's
not the only problem because - so the fact

00:57:02.140 --> 00:57:05.819
that it’s an infinite search is very bad
for you because there's no way you can calculate

00:57:05.819 --> 00:57:09.630
how much time and energy it’s going to take
you to solve the problem.

00:57:09.630 --> 00:57:13.069
So, that’s stressful.

00:57:13.069 --> 00:57:14.220
Why?

00:57:14.220 --> 00:57:15.392
Well obviously it’s stressful.

00:57:15.392 --> 00:57:21.580
It means you have an infinite journey towards
an uncertain goal, and you can't even handle

00:57:21.580 --> 00:57:23.330
that you know.

00:57:23.330 --> 00:57:27.549
I mean, your journeys have to be such that
they're self sustaining right?

00:57:27.549 --> 00:57:32.900
I mean, generally speaking, when you're on
a journey of some sort, you have to obtain

00:57:32.900 --> 00:57:40.869
as much matter and energy as the journey costs,
and maybe a bit more because you're storing

00:57:40.869 --> 00:57:42.460
some up for the future.

00:57:42.460 --> 00:57:49.599
But if you've got this path of infinite complexity
laying out in front of you, it’s like your

00:57:49.599 --> 00:57:55.250
body can't calibrate that and so what it does
is it throws you into emergency reaction mode

00:57:55.250 --> 00:58:02.410
and emergency reaction mode is prepare for
everything, and that's not good.

00:58:02.410 --> 00:58:10.170
And so, down there in the underworld the world
itself turns into a maze of infinite complexity,

00:58:10.170 --> 00:58:17.599
but you turn into a mass of competing models,
and a lot of them are based in very low level

00:58:17.599 --> 00:58:18.599
motivation.

00:58:18.599 --> 00:58:19.599
You're going to be angry.

00:58:19.599 --> 00:58:20.599
That’s for sure.

00:58:20.599 --> 00:58:23.890
You might be angry god only knows for how
long, you know, if it’s a particularly nasty

00:58:23.890 --> 00:58:24.890
disease.

00:58:24.890 --> 00:58:30.040
You might be angry for the rest of your life,
and you know, you can be angry in a small

00:58:30.040 --> 00:58:36.289
way and accept fate, or you can be so angry
that it completely consumes you and you're

00:58:36.289 --> 00:58:41.740
out for revenge against being itself, and
that happens to people, and that's only one

00:58:41.740 --> 00:58:48.410
of the emotions and motivational states that
are going to be hyper activated by the response,

00:58:48.410 --> 00:58:57.089
and so what happens is that your system collapses
into the underlying basic motivational states

00:58:57.089 --> 00:59:03.390
and so you're anxious, and afraid, and angry,
and exploratory, and upset, and all of these

00:59:03.390 --> 00:59:07.880
things all at the same time, and that is your
reaction to the unknown.

00:59:07.880 --> 00:59:13.609
Your reaction to the unknown is stop, because
you aren't where you thought you were, and

00:59:13.609 --> 00:59:18.849
then the next thing is prepare for anything,
and the way you prepare for anything is by

00:59:18.849 --> 00:59:21.920
getting ready to do whatever it is that you
can do.

00:59:21.920 --> 00:59:28.630
So, I kinda think about it as you’re in
a car and you've got the gas pedal pushed

00:59:28.630 --> 00:59:33.440
right to the metal and you've got the break
on at the same time, so the car’s roaring

00:59:33.440 --> 00:59:38.410
away but - and you know if you take the foot
off the break, man it’s gonna move, but

00:59:38.410 --> 00:59:41.180
your foot isn't off the break because you
don't know where to go but you're still running

00:59:41.180 --> 00:59:45.980
at full tilt, and that's not psychological
exactly right.

00:59:45.980 --> 00:59:52.370
It’s psychophysiological because you’re
an energy and material burning system, and

00:59:52.370 --> 00:59:56.900
if you crank yourself up into high gear then
you're going to be burning a lot more energy

00:59:56.900 --> 01:00:01.400
and material, and what happens to people is
that well one of the things that happens is

01:00:01.400 --> 01:00:06.500
that you’ll shut your immune system off
because if you're being - because it’s costly

01:00:06.500 --> 01:00:10.480
- if you're being chased by a tiger, who the
hell cares if you get the flu in two weeks.

01:00:10.480 --> 01:00:12.710
It’s like shut that thing off.

01:00:12.710 --> 01:00:17.779
Everything that has to do with long term survival,
even your ability to think into the long term,

01:00:17.779 --> 01:00:25.720
is going to be shut off because your ability
to think in the long term failed, and so you

01:00:25.720 --> 01:00:32.010
default back down to instantaneous impulsive
modes of actions, and it’s the right thing

01:00:32.010 --> 01:00:33.010
to do.

01:00:33.010 --> 01:00:37.289
You might think, “Oh well this is a hell
of a time to have a cloudy mind,” but - and

01:00:37.289 --> 01:00:41.809
sometimes that's the case - but you're wired
up.

01:00:41.809 --> 01:00:45.109
You're trying to solve this impossible problem
which is what the hell do you do when you

01:00:45.109 --> 01:00:51.930
don't know what to do, and the only answer
to that is prepare for everything because

01:00:51.930 --> 01:00:55.180
you're going to gather some more information
and that might tell you what needs to be done

01:00:55.180 --> 01:00:58.339
next but you have no idea how fast it’s
going to have to be done.

01:00:58.339 --> 01:01:04.760
It might be like you need to do this right
now or you don't get it don't at all.

01:01:04.760 --> 01:01:08.849
So then you're down there in the underworld
where all the, you know, great motivational

01:01:08.849 --> 01:01:13.660
beasts lurk and where there's an infinite
path in front of you that's - and maybe you

01:01:13.660 --> 01:01:16.809
don't get out, and that's the thing too.

01:01:16.809 --> 01:01:23.980
The thing is that this isn't psychological,
and one of the real problems with modern clinical

01:01:23.980 --> 01:01:30.950
psychology and modern psychiatry - just conceptually
- is that people automatically assume that

01:01:30.950 --> 01:01:37.500
if you have a problem, like if you're depressed
and unhappy and anxious, well then that’s

01:01:37.500 --> 01:01:38.890
a mental problem.

01:01:38.890 --> 01:01:40.359
It’s a psychological problem.

01:01:40.359 --> 01:01:47.930
It’s like it’s a problem insofar as it’s
not pleasant, but what if you're in a horrible

01:01:47.930 --> 01:01:49.059
situation?

01:01:49.059 --> 01:01:52.309
Well you know, the cognitive people would
say, “Well you know, you just have to reconstrue

01:01:52.309 --> 01:01:53.309
it.”

01:01:53.309 --> 01:01:57.030
It’s like yeah if you can reconstrue it
man, you're not in a horrible situation, you

01:01:57.030 --> 01:02:01.279
know, and one of the things you see with the
cognitive behaviorists for example is when

01:02:01.279 --> 01:02:04.950
they assess depression, they use the Beck
Depression Inventory, and I’ve given the

01:02:04.950 --> 01:02:12.359
Beck Depression Inventory to hundreds of people,
and their criteria for when you should be

01:02:12.359 --> 01:02:18.119
an outpatient in a psychiatric ward is probably
the level of depression that one-third of

01:02:18.119 --> 01:02:23.289
you experienced the first year that you were
in university cause I've given this instrument

01:02:23.289 --> 01:02:27.880
to lots of university students and one-third
of them, at least in the first year, meet

01:02:27.880 --> 01:02:30.310
criteria for hospitalization with the Beck.

01:02:30.310 --> 01:02:38.010
So the way that cognitive behaviorist people
- what do you call - evade this issue is that

01:02:38.010 --> 01:02:41.809
they just set their diagnostic instrument
so low that the people that they described

01:02:41.809 --> 01:02:47.150
as depressed actually don't have a problem,
you know, so I think the cutoff for outpatient

01:02:47.150 --> 01:02:48.390
is fifteen.

01:02:48.390 --> 01:02:52.369
You know, you're not happy when you get a
fifteen on the Beck, but if you're depressed

01:02:52.369 --> 01:02:53.589
you get a sixty.

01:02:53.589 --> 01:02:58.029
It’s like you're depressed, and then you
talk to someone like that and you find out

01:02:58.029 --> 01:03:02.640
well maybe you're depressed or maybe you just
have a terrible life, and the terrible life

01:03:02.640 --> 01:03:06.349
is - and good luck reconstruing that man.

01:03:06.349 --> 01:03:12.970
So you're not very bright, for example, and
that's a big problem so maybe you have an

01:03:12.970 --> 01:03:14.800
IQ of 85, or maybe you have an IQ of 80.

01:03:14.800 --> 01:03:17.570
It’s like good luck finding a job, and if
you do find one, it’s not gonna pay, and

01:03:17.570 --> 01:03:23.970
it’s not going to be stable, and you're
not going to get good hours, and so then well

01:03:23.970 --> 01:03:29.760
you know your only friend is crazy, and then
your family is like distorted and twisted

01:03:29.760 --> 01:03:33.830
up beyond belief so maybe you know, your mother
is chronic alcoholic with dementia.

01:03:33.830 --> 01:03:40.990
That’d be a nice touch, and you know, maybe
your friends aren't only not very good for

01:03:40.990 --> 01:03:45.619
you, they're actively bad for you because
you've fallen in with some bloody psychopaths,

01:03:45.619 --> 01:03:50.250
and because you're not particularly bright,
they can get your money every time you get

01:03:50.250 --> 01:03:55.230
your support check, your unemployment check
or something like that.

01:03:55.230 --> 01:03:59.460
Well you know, you get someone like that in
your practice and you think, well they're

01:03:59.460 --> 01:04:00.460
unhappy.

01:04:00.460 --> 01:04:02.079
Are they depressed?

01:04:02.079 --> 01:04:03.380
Well, no.

01:04:03.380 --> 01:04:04.930
They're in the underworld.

01:04:04.930 --> 01:04:07.119
It’s not the same thing.

01:04:07.119 --> 01:04:09.099
Here’s depressed.

01:04:09.099 --> 01:04:11.470
Depressed is someone comes in.

01:04:11.470 --> 01:04:13.330
They're reasonably well educated.

01:04:13.330 --> 01:04:17.140
They have a job, and when you ask them about
the job, they say, “Well you know, I used

01:04:17.140 --> 01:04:21.019
to like my job” or maybe “I even do like
my job but I just don't find that I'm motivated

01:04:21.019 --> 01:04:23.380
right now, and I can't understand why.”

01:04:23.380 --> 01:04:27.420
Then you ask them about their marriage and
they say, “Well you know, I have a good

01:04:27.420 --> 01:04:29.660
marriage” and then you ask them about their
family and they say, “Well, I’ve got the

01:04:29.660 --> 01:04:32.980
average number of family problems but it’s,
you know, all things considered it’s not

01:04:32.980 --> 01:04:34.299
too bad.”

01:04:34.299 --> 01:04:38.850
And so, and then they don't have a drug or
alcohol problem, but they score like thirty

01:04:38.850 --> 01:04:39.850
on the Beck.

01:04:39.850 --> 01:04:42.000
You think, “Hey, you can take some antidepressants.

01:04:42.000 --> 01:04:43.170
You're depressed.

01:04:43.170 --> 01:04:44.960
You don't have a terrible life.”

01:04:44.960 --> 01:04:45.960
You know, and they know that.

01:04:45.960 --> 01:04:50.289
They often don't have any real cognitive symptoms
either, although they might be reacting more

01:04:50.289 --> 01:04:53.019
negatively to some of the negative things
that are happening.

01:04:53.019 --> 01:04:57.210
With those people, something’s gone astray,
you know, maybe their sleep is upset.

01:04:57.210 --> 01:04:58.730
That’s often the case.

01:04:58.730 --> 01:05:06.190
So and what they're suffering from is disequilibrium
of sleep wake rhythms, so it’s gone out

01:05:06.190 --> 01:05:07.190
of whack.

01:05:07.190 --> 01:05:10.630
So you straighten up their sleep and they're
better, or sometimes they need antidepressants,

01:05:10.630 --> 01:05:15.789
but for the first person, like antidepressants
might take the edge off and maybe not too,

01:05:15.789 --> 01:05:17.819
but - and what are you gonna do?

01:05:17.819 --> 01:05:20.180
You're gonna get the person to reconstrue?

01:05:20.180 --> 01:05:24.150
That assumes they have options, you know,
and the problem with educated smart people

01:05:24.150 --> 01:05:26.569
is they think everybody has options because
they do.

01:05:26.569 --> 01:05:28.450
It’s like no, that’s not right.

01:05:28.450 --> 01:05:31.810
Some people have - some people have no options.

01:05:31.810 --> 01:05:36.750
They're like trapped in a corner, and no matter
how hard you try, you cannot get them out

01:05:36.750 --> 01:05:38.369
of the corner.

01:05:38.369 --> 01:05:41.420
Sometimes it takes a virtual miracle before
that can happen.

01:05:41.420 --> 01:05:44.220
So the thing about the underworld is that
it’s a real place.

01:05:44.220 --> 01:05:50.890
It’s a real place, and - depending of course
on how you define place, but that's exactly

01:05:50.890 --> 01:05:51.890
the issue.

01:05:51.890 --> 01:05:54.049
How should you define place?

01:05:54.049 --> 01:06:00.410
Well, I would say for the sake of argument
that you might as well define it two ways.

01:06:00.410 --> 01:06:04.790
You might as well use the standard Newtonian
view, you know, that the world’s made out

01:06:04.790 --> 01:06:10.180
of inanimate material entities, arrayed in
a certain manner, because that's a very useful

01:06:10.180 --> 01:06:11.980
approach to many problems.

01:06:11.980 --> 01:06:16.430
But then there's another perspective which
is no, no, no, the meanings that array themselves

01:06:16.430 --> 01:06:18.910
in front of you in the world - those are real.

01:06:18.910 --> 01:06:23.940
And you know, I would also say - I’ll just
mention this very briefly.

01:06:23.940 --> 01:06:35.000
It’s something the idea I’ve been working
on for a long time is that there’s an implication

01:06:35.000 --> 01:06:42.310
of Darwinian theory, and the implication is
that - we talked about this a little bit - the

01:06:42.310 --> 01:06:50.509
environment is very complex and it’s transforming
unpredictably, and so what that means is you

01:06:50.509 --> 01:06:52.730
can never be right about the environment.

01:06:52.730 --> 01:06:55.599
So right, correct is out of reach.

01:06:55.599 --> 01:07:02.290
The best you can do is come to partial - you
can partially accommodate it, and so you'd

01:07:02.290 --> 01:07:04.569
say okay well how have we partially accommodated
it?

01:07:04.569 --> 01:07:05.670
Okay so here we are.

01:07:05.670 --> 01:07:09.490
We’ve been evolving for three and a half
billion years, given it our best shot, and

01:07:09.490 --> 01:07:15.000
here we have these bodies, and they're out
solution to the problems posed by the transforming

01:07:15.000 --> 01:07:18.130
environment, and you might say, “Well how
good a solution are those?”

01:07:18.130 --> 01:07:20.289
Well, you've got seventy years.

01:07:20.289 --> 01:07:24.809
It’s the best we’ve been able to manage
in three and a half billion years is to generate

01:07:24.809 --> 01:07:29.059
up a biological platform that has enough flexibility
to keep up with the environment for about

01:07:29.059 --> 01:07:31.089
75 years.

01:07:31.089 --> 01:07:35.460
So then you think, so there's no final answer
to the problem the environment presents.

01:07:35.460 --> 01:07:40.519
There isn't, and it’s because it actually
changes unpredictably.

01:07:40.519 --> 01:07:42.869
So there's no way of being right.

01:07:42.869 --> 01:07:45.359
Now then you might say, well what does right
mean?

01:07:45.359 --> 01:07:50.960
Well, from the Darwinian perspective, from
some sense there's no other right than that

01:07:50.960 --> 01:07:54.640
you're alive and you're likely to reproduce.

01:07:54.640 --> 01:07:56.339
That's the best you ever get.

01:07:56.339 --> 01:08:01.050
Okay so let’s say that's your pragmatic
definition of right.

01:08:01.050 --> 01:08:06.809
Well then what that means is if that is right,
as far as right can be determined, as far

01:08:06.809 --> 01:08:16.160
as being human is concerned, then when you
are reacting by fleeing or freezing when something

01:08:16.160 --> 01:08:21.130
terrible is chasing you because you perceive
it as a threat, the fact that it’s a threat

01:08:21.130 --> 01:08:22.580
is right.

01:08:22.580 --> 01:08:25.500
It’s not an overlay on reality.

01:08:25.500 --> 01:08:30.620
It’s like if you didn't perceive a predator
as a threat, then you would get eaten, and

01:08:30.620 --> 01:08:37.080
so I think - you know, you can think of the
Darwinian world as nested inside the Newtonian

01:08:37.080 --> 01:08:39.960
world which is how most scientists think,
but I don't believe that.

01:08:39.960 --> 01:08:41.770
I think it’s the other way around.

01:08:41.770 --> 01:08:47.390
I think the Darwinian world is the ultimate
world in a sense, and the Newtonian world

01:08:47.390 --> 01:08:50.620
is just a tiny subset of that and it’s a
useful subset.

01:08:50.620 --> 01:08:51.620
It’s a tool.

01:08:51.620 --> 01:08:57.570
It’s useful subset, but the real world - the
Darwin takes precedence over all knowledge

01:08:57.570 --> 01:08:58.570
structures.

01:08:58.570 --> 01:09:03.370
That’s how it looks to me, and I don't see
a way out of that argument.

01:09:03.370 --> 01:09:05.790
So, I mean it depends to a large - Yup.

01:09:05.790 --> 01:09:39.931
So are you saying that - cause I’m trying
to understand what you're saying.

01:09:39.931 --> 01:09:40.931
Are you saying that like our estimations of
like the deterministic, material world is

01:09:40.931 --> 01:09:41.931
nested in an evolutionary drive to find truth
so that we can make quick assumptions about

01:09:41.931 --> 01:09:42.931
our environment?

01:09:42.931 --> 01:09:43.931
Yeah, so well here's another example.

01:09:43.931 --> 01:09:44.931
You guys can think about this for a minute
if you'd like.

01:09:44.931 --> 01:09:51.850
So, let’s say that one of the logical consequences
of pursuing science from a Newtonian perspective

01:09:51.850 --> 01:09:57.400
is that you learn how to create - cross ebola
with smallpox which is what the KGB was trying

01:09:57.400 --> 01:09:58.450
to do in the 1980s.

01:09:58.450 --> 01:10:00.810
Okay, so you manage that and everyone dies.

01:10:00.810 --> 01:10:04.230
It’s like, how’s that for a validation
of science?

01:10:04.230 --> 01:10:09.280
You know, it’s like - because this could
happen right?

01:10:09.280 --> 01:10:15.070
Our technological capability which is fostered
by this particular model could easily enable

01:10:15.070 --> 01:10:17.750
us to produce something that will wipe all
of us out.

01:10:17.750 --> 01:10:23.240
And then well are you gonna say, “Well yeah
apart from that, it was right.”

01:10:23.240 --> 01:10:29.020
And then I’d think it’s a tricky argument
because okay let's say - but here's part of

01:10:29.020 --> 01:10:32.070
the problem, and I also thought the same thing
about hydrogen bombs.

01:10:32.070 --> 01:10:37.340
It’s like well obviously the quantum view,
let’s say, is accurate because if you follow

01:10:37.340 --> 01:10:39.190
it you can make a hydrogen bomb.

01:10:39.190 --> 01:10:43.970
But then you can step back from that and you
can say yeah, yeah, but to make a hydrogen

01:10:43.970 --> 01:10:49.300
bomb or to cross ebola with smallpox, you
have to treat the matter that you're interacting

01:10:49.300 --> 01:10:54.930
with as if it’s actually separate from everything
else, and the thing is it’s not actually

01:10:54.930 --> 01:10:56.630
separate from everything else.

01:10:56.630 --> 01:10:59.160
It’s actually connected to everything else.

01:10:59.160 --> 01:11:03.610
Now you can say, well if you treat it as if
it’s separate, here’s some operations

01:11:03.610 --> 01:11:04.700
you can undertake.

01:11:04.700 --> 01:11:08.440
You can cross ebola with smallpox, but then
you’ve ignored a bunch of things like maybe

01:11:08.440 --> 01:11:11.410
that's not a good idea, and then everyone
dies.

01:11:11.410 --> 01:11:16.510
And so the reductionism in the model which
helps it specify its target and increase its

01:11:16.510 --> 01:11:22.950
accuracy has the corresponding negative consequence
of pretending that the context within which

01:11:22.950 --> 01:11:28.910
these enterprises are being conducted can
be ignored and that that’s actually appropriate,

01:11:28.910 --> 01:11:29.910
and that's a big problem.

01:11:29.910 --> 01:11:31.380
It’s a big problem.

01:11:31.380 --> 01:11:38.470
So the evolutionary framework provides the
concepts because it provides like our sense

01:11:38.470 --> 01:11:39.470
of morality.

01:11:39.470 --> 01:11:40.960
Well, it’s not only because of what it provides
us.

01:11:40.960 --> 01:11:49.190
I think the reason that the evolutionary perspective
has to be the one that supersedes is because

01:11:49.190 --> 01:11:51.140
you cannot model the environment.

01:11:51.140 --> 01:11:52.870
It’s not possible.

01:11:52.870 --> 01:11:57.580
All you can do is keep up with it, and the
only way you can keep up with it is through

01:11:57.580 --> 01:11:59.320
Darwinian processes - random.

01:11:59.320 --> 01:12:04.230
I mean I know it’s not a complete theory,
but for the sake of argument, you have to

01:12:04.230 --> 01:12:10.310
generate random alterations, and you know,
you can say well in principle, we can understand

01:12:10.310 --> 01:12:11.330
everything scientifically.

01:12:11.330 --> 01:12:14.600
It’s like no, in principle, we can't.

01:12:14.600 --> 01:12:16.310
Everything's too complex to understand.

01:12:16.310 --> 01:12:24.390
So that's why Newtonian physics is nested
within a Darwinian perspective.

01:12:24.390 --> 01:12:25.390
That’s right.

01:12:25.390 --> 01:12:29.920
Because it’s just an estimation whereas
a Darwinian perspective, it’s like it’s

01:12:29.920 --> 01:12:31.090
more in line with what’s actually -
It’s more in line with reality.

01:12:31.090 --> 01:12:32.090
Yeah, whereas the Newtonian perspective comes
out of a rough estimation of what reality

01:12:32.090 --> 01:12:41.100
is like.

01:12:41.100 --> 01:12:47.560
Well Jung, when Jung was talking about the
origin of the scientific mindset, you know,

01:12:47.560 --> 01:12:50.640
he traced its development back to alchemy.

01:12:50.640 --> 01:12:55.650
Here’s another reason why the scientific
mindset is necessarily nested in the mythological

01:12:55.650 --> 01:12:56.650
mindset.

01:12:56.650 --> 01:13:03.750
So what the alchemists were trying to do is
mess around with matter and to produce something.

01:13:03.750 --> 01:13:07.790
They argued for centuries what this thing
was.

01:13:07.790 --> 01:13:14.310
This philosopher’s stone, and what the philosopher’s
stone would enable the holder of it to do

01:13:14.310 --> 01:13:20.660
would be to transmute elements into gold,
so that would make you rich, and it would

01:13:20.660 --> 01:13:29.160
provide immortality and perfect health for
the person who discovered it.

01:13:29.160 --> 01:13:32.530
Okay, now what Jung said - and we’ll talk
about this.

01:13:32.530 --> 01:13:36.160
If I have enough energy, we’ll talk about
this two-thirds of the way through class because

01:13:36.160 --> 01:13:40.240
it’s so damn complicated that some years
I can't muster up enough energy to discuss

01:13:40.240 --> 01:13:48.940
it, but Jung believed that, you know, people
have always been trying to cope with their

01:13:48.940 --> 01:13:50.460
mortality, right, and their vulnerability.

01:13:50.460 --> 01:13:52.460
Okay, fine, and that makes perfect sense.

01:13:52.460 --> 01:13:58.290
So he did an analysis of Western history,
roughly speaking, and said well what happened

01:13:58.290 --> 01:14:03.300
- there was a Christian epoch, maybe, that
lasted around 1,600 years, roughly speaking,

01:14:03.300 --> 01:14:11.590
and there was an idea at the base of it and
the idea was that through spiritual discipline,

01:14:11.590 --> 01:14:17.830
man could transcend suffering, but - the problem
with that was that all this suffering kept

01:14:17.830 --> 01:14:22.430
happening, and you know, and there was some
warps in the idea, as Nietzsche pointed out

01:14:22.430 --> 01:14:29.140
how this ceasing of suffering was only going
to happen after you die.

01:14:29.140 --> 01:14:34.590
Okay fine, then you live forever in bliss,
but still there's like black plague and babies

01:14:34.590 --> 01:14:38.770
dying and crops failing, and it’s like it
just doesn't seem that you can just ignore

01:14:38.770 --> 01:14:44.750
that, and so you've got 1,500 or 1,600 years
of Christianity, and you know, the whole history

01:14:44.750 --> 01:14:49.770
of Christianity behind that grinding away
on this problem trying to produce a system

01:14:49.770 --> 01:14:56.120
of redemption, and it doesn't work or it works
partially.

01:14:56.120 --> 01:14:57.790
And it also ignores certain things.

01:14:57.790 --> 01:15:02.210
So partly what it ignores, you know, one of
the presuppositions of, say, medieval Christianity

01:15:02.210 --> 01:15:08.480
is that the world, roughly speaking, is an
evil place, and that matter is corrupt and

01:15:08.480 --> 01:15:09.960
unworthy of attention.

01:15:09.960 --> 01:15:14.110
Well, one of Jung’s hypothesis is that if
you ignore something long enough, then it

01:15:14.110 --> 01:15:17.460
starts to demand your attention, you know.

01:15:17.460 --> 01:15:23.070
It’s like a repression, and then that's
especially the case if there's a problem that

01:15:23.070 --> 01:15:26.500
you're trying to solve that your current worldview
isn't really solving.

01:15:26.500 --> 01:15:31.850
It’s like your attention is going to be
drawn unconsciously to that which you've ignored

01:15:31.850 --> 01:15:38.640
and put down, and so Jung saw the development
of alchemy as a parallel to the development

01:15:38.640 --> 01:15:44.730
of Christianity, but alchemy studied matter,
which was forbidden in a sense.

01:15:44.730 --> 01:15:47.600
It was damned from the Christian perspective.

01:15:47.600 --> 01:15:51.320
The alchemists were studying the transmutations
of matter.

01:15:51.320 --> 01:15:54.470
They had an intuition that there was information
locked in that.

01:15:54.470 --> 01:15:55.640
In matter.

01:15:55.640 --> 01:15:59.010
And that if you could just figure out how
to interact with it properly, then you can

01:15:59.010 --> 01:16:03.680
free up the information, and they symbolized
that in the dreamlike manner.

01:16:03.680 --> 01:16:07.020
The dreamlike manner was you could discover
the philosopher’s stone and that would give

01:16:07.020 --> 01:16:11.070
you, you know, wealth, health, and eternal
life.

01:16:11.070 --> 01:16:17.240
Well as far as Jung was concerned, that was
a dream, and it was like an artistic production.

01:16:17.240 --> 01:16:22.210
It was like the scaffolding of the scientific
perspective because it isn't like people went

01:16:22.210 --> 01:16:26.400
from medieval Christianity to full fledged
scientists like overnight.

01:16:26.400 --> 01:16:30.190
It took hundreds of years for this to occur.

01:16:30.190 --> 01:16:34.460
There were changes in the substrata of thought
that were occurring as this was happening

01:16:34.460 --> 01:16:39.800
and Jung traced that back more than 1,600
years in the development of alchemy.

01:16:39.800 --> 01:16:44.710
And so, the dream of alchemy was that you
could discover - you could master the transmutations

01:16:44.710 --> 01:16:49.270
of matter such that there would be no disease,
that no one would die, and everyone was wealthy.

01:16:49.270 --> 01:16:55.640
Well okay that, as far as Jung was concerned,
that provided the motivation for science because

01:16:55.640 --> 01:16:59.120
one of the things you might ask is well, why
do people do science.

01:16:59.120 --> 01:17:02.060
I mean, most people never have right.

01:17:02.060 --> 01:17:03.310
It’s not very old.

01:17:03.310 --> 01:17:04.500
Six hundred years.

01:17:04.500 --> 01:17:06.570
It only emerged in one place, roughly speaking.

01:17:06.570 --> 01:17:12.410
It’s very fragile, and unlikely, and plus
just think of what you have to do if you're

01:17:12.410 --> 01:17:13.410
a scientist.

01:17:13.410 --> 01:17:18.140
You know, if you're a scientist and you use
a microscope - a high powered microscope - you

01:17:18.140 --> 01:17:22.810
have to spend ten thousand hours looking through
that damn thing before you can see what's

01:17:22.810 --> 01:17:27.900
there, and so you think that’s a weird thing
for a tree dwelling ape to be doing.

01:17:27.900 --> 01:17:32.640
You know what I mean, it’s such a warp of
our natural, of our natural instincts and

01:17:32.640 --> 01:17:37.170
our, you know, our desire to make contact
with other people and to be doing something

01:17:37.170 --> 01:17:41.410
exciting, and you're looking through this
damn tube, you know, and it’s even painful.

01:17:41.410 --> 01:17:43.850
What the hell is motivating you?

01:17:43.850 --> 01:17:48.020
And the answer would be, well you think you're
doing something good.

01:17:48.020 --> 01:17:50.520
You think that science is going to benefit
mankind.

01:17:50.520 --> 01:17:53.570
So it’s nested necessarily in a motivated
system.

01:17:53.570 --> 01:17:56.740
It isn't that you do science because you want
to discover facts.

01:17:56.740 --> 01:18:01.060
It’s that you do science to discover facts
so that things improve.

01:18:01.060 --> 01:18:03.270
Isn't it?

01:18:03.270 --> 01:18:09.600
And you just can't ignore the fact that you're
doing it in the hopes that things will improve.

01:18:09.600 --> 01:18:13.800
You can't just take that out of the equation
and say, “No I’m purely objective.”

01:18:13.800 --> 01:18:19.580
It’s like A, you're not and you better not
be, you know, because a purely objective person

01:18:19.580 --> 01:18:21.860
wouldn't be a person.

01:18:21.860 --> 01:18:24.010
You couldn't even communicate with someone
like that.

01:18:24.010 --> 01:18:27.230
Plus they wouldn't be able to - someone like
that wouldn't be able to determine what was

01:18:27.230 --> 01:18:31.670
relevant - distinguish what was relevant and
what wasn't relevant.

01:18:31.670 --> 01:18:34.230
You need motivational systems to do that,
and I can tell you.

01:18:34.230 --> 01:18:39.030
When you're looking at a data set, there's
an infinite number of things that you can

01:18:39.030 --> 01:18:40.470
look at in that data set.

01:18:40.470 --> 01:18:41.470
Literally.

01:18:41.470 --> 01:18:46.110
I mean there's no end to the statistical procedures
that you can implement.

01:18:46.110 --> 01:18:49.480
It’s like fiddling with a photo with Photoshop.

01:18:49.480 --> 01:18:53.830
It’s like man, you can fiddle with that
damn thing for the rest of your life, and

01:18:53.830 --> 01:19:00.460
so what that means is that the complexity
that’s there can't be dealt with objectively

01:19:00.460 --> 01:19:06.200
because if you deal with it objectively, there's
no way of separating the one path through

01:19:06.200 --> 01:19:09.061
the data from all the other millions of paths
through the data.

01:19:09.061 --> 01:19:12.480
It’s not like when you're doing statistics
man, it’s not like you take a bunch of numbers

01:19:12.480 --> 01:19:15.010
and you feed it through the meat grinder and
out comes the results.

01:19:15.010 --> 01:19:17.310
It is absolutely nothing like that.

01:19:17.310 --> 01:19:22.449
You're making ethical choices at every single
level of the decision, and you know, if you're

01:19:22.449 --> 01:19:24.470
- and I teach my students this too.

01:19:24.470 --> 01:19:28.540
So I want them to be straight scientists,
and there's lots of things that can warp you

01:19:28.540 --> 01:19:32.700
as a scientist, and one can be you want to
have a career, and the other is you want to

01:19:32.700 --> 01:19:38.410
climb up the primate, the scientist primate
dominance hierarchy, and you know fair enough.

01:19:38.410 --> 01:19:43.370
You need to have a career, and maybe the ambition
is worth it, but the problem with that is

01:19:43.370 --> 01:19:48.870
that can make you do things that violate the
principles of the entire enterprise, and that's

01:19:48.870 --> 01:19:52.770
happening - I mean it’s been scandalous
particularly in social psychology because

01:19:52.770 --> 01:19:56.480
people do what’s called data mining and
they just keep doing their analysis until

01:19:56.480 --> 01:20:00.940
something significant pops up and then they
write a story about why that’s important.

01:20:00.940 --> 01:20:02.760
I’m not cynical about that.

01:20:02.760 --> 01:20:04.050
I understand why it happens.

01:20:04.050 --> 01:20:08.870
I think it’s inevitable that it happens
some because lots of people who are publishing,

01:20:08.870 --> 01:20:11.680
it’s like the first bloody thing or second
thing they've ever published.

01:20:11.680 --> 01:20:13.850
They don't know what they're doing.

01:20:13.850 --> 01:20:16.140
There's gonna be error like mad.

01:20:16.140 --> 01:20:17.880
But I tell my students, “Look.

01:20:17.880 --> 01:20:22.740
If you're going to do statistical analysis
properly, you have to get yourself oriented.

01:20:22.740 --> 01:20:24.830
What the hell are you doing?

01:20:24.830 --> 01:20:27.030
What is it that you're really doing?”

01:20:27.030 --> 01:20:30.570
And you think well I'm trying to discover
something solid, and the reason I’m trying

01:20:30.570 --> 01:20:34.390
to do that is because there should be benefits
from that otherwise the whole enterprise is

01:20:34.390 --> 01:20:38.240
foolish, and you might as well be a bloody
- you might as well go work in finance if

01:20:38.240 --> 01:20:42.350
you're not interested in that because - really,
you're not going to make any money as a scientist

01:20:42.350 --> 01:20:46.540
and you can make lots of money in finance,
and you're smart enough to go into finance.

01:20:46.540 --> 01:20:50.970
So, if that's what you want - if you want
power and money, and I'm not complaining about

01:20:50.970 --> 01:20:53.010
that, it’s like go where it is.

01:20:53.010 --> 01:20:54.750
But science isn't it.

01:20:54.750 --> 01:21:00.060
If you want to discover facts, maybe, and
that maybe of some benefit at some point,

01:21:00.060 --> 01:21:01.520
then you become a scientist.

01:21:01.520 --> 01:21:06.280
Okay, so that you gotta keep your eye on the
ball, and then you have to decide if pursuing

01:21:06.280 --> 01:21:11.550
that line of truth and benefit supersedes
whatever you might do to build your career.

01:21:11.550 --> 01:21:14.210
Okay, cause you're always playing that game.

01:21:14.210 --> 01:21:15.210
Always.

01:21:15.210 --> 01:21:19.600
Your whole life, and that's actually why so
much of scientific research is wrong.

01:21:19.600 --> 01:21:23.330
There's error too, and there's all sorts of
other reasons, and you can't expect a system

01:21:23.330 --> 01:21:27.710
to do much better than produce five percent
of useful output anyways.

01:21:27.710 --> 01:21:33.230
So, okay and then the other thing is you have
to also be afraid, and here's what you should

01:21:33.230 --> 01:21:34.290
be afraid of.

01:21:34.290 --> 01:21:37.450
Let’s say that you discover something that
isn't there.

01:21:37.450 --> 01:21:41.180
Okay, then you're gonna spend the next fifteen
years studying it.

01:21:41.180 --> 01:21:45.530
So if you wanna spend your next fifteen years
studying this thing that you've conjured out

01:21:45.530 --> 01:21:47.170
of nothing, then go right ahead.

01:21:47.170 --> 01:21:50.700
But if you don't want to do that, then you
better be properly oriented when you do your

01:21:50.700 --> 01:21:56.250
statistical analysis, and that's because statistical
analysis is an ethical process, and so is

01:21:56.250 --> 01:21:59.050
science because it has to be.

01:21:59.050 --> 01:22:05.960
If it isn't nested inside ethics, fundamentally,
then god only knows.

01:22:05.960 --> 01:22:07.430
It can be completely destructive.

01:22:07.430 --> 01:22:09.130
It could be counterproductive.

01:22:09.130 --> 01:22:16.110
Like, so - ah so then there's a variety of
reasons why the Darwinian bucket is sort of

01:22:16.110 --> 01:22:18.400
- it’s the outside.

01:22:18.400 --> 01:22:22.100
You don't get any farther out than that as
far as I can tell.

01:22:22.100 --> 01:22:26.820
So, and it’s the Darwinian world to which
we’re adapted, you know.

01:22:26.820 --> 01:22:28.610
That's why we perceive things as threats.

01:22:28.610 --> 01:22:30.730
That’s why we perceive things as positive.

01:22:30.730 --> 01:22:32.820
That's why we perceive things as negative.

01:22:32.820 --> 01:22:36.340
You know, and we tend to think those are overlays
on a deeper reality.

01:22:36.340 --> 01:22:43.060
It’s like that is not so clear, and you
know, I didn't know this but when I was working

01:22:43.060 --> 01:22:49.490
out these ideas, I had never read anything
that Heidegger had written although his influences

01:22:49.490 --> 01:22:53.810
wormed its way into many fields and I undoubtedly
picked up some of his ideas, you know, because

01:22:53.810 --> 01:22:58.140
they get embedded in other places, but certainly
one of Heidegger’s claims was that Western

01:22:58.140 --> 01:23:04.430
philosophy got off on the wrong foot right
at the beginning because it didn't study being.

01:23:04.430 --> 01:23:10.320
It started to think about thought, and what’s
- the real issue is being what is being?

01:23:10.320 --> 01:23:15.260
Well being is what you experience, and for
the Heideggerians, that’s reality.

01:23:15.260 --> 01:23:18.270
There isn't a reality underneath that.

01:23:18.270 --> 01:23:22.810
Now, it requires a real shift in perspective
to start to try to think that way, and I think

01:23:22.810 --> 01:23:26.290
the easiest way to do it is to think both
ways, you know.

01:23:26.290 --> 01:23:27.540
Sometimes you think like a Newtonian.

01:23:27.540 --> 01:23:30.100
Fair enough, and then other times you don't.

01:23:30.100 --> 01:23:33.760
And you need to know when to think like a
Newtonian, and when to think like someone

01:23:33.760 --> 01:23:41.790
who understands that the phenomena of meaning
that surrounds us are legitimate, and it’s

01:23:41.790 --> 01:23:45.740
a very interesting thing, and this is one
of the things that's really driven me through

01:23:45.740 --> 01:23:53.160
this material is okay so we can make a case
that like lower order meanings are real.

01:23:53.160 --> 01:24:00.580
Like if you perceive a bear or a cliff as
a threat, then that's so you don't die, and

01:24:00.580 --> 01:24:04.140
it’s worked really well for things that
have been chased by bears for ever since there's

01:24:04.140 --> 01:24:10.250
been bears, and way before that.

01:24:10.250 --> 01:24:12.560
But then there’s another question that emerges.

01:24:12.560 --> 01:24:14.170
So okay, here’s the question.

01:24:14.170 --> 01:24:19.910
So, you've got all these underlying motivational
systems that are built into you as a consequence

01:24:19.910 --> 01:24:21.810
of evolutionary processes.

01:24:21.810 --> 01:24:27.370
So let’s say it’s your body and the manifestation
of your body in your brain, in your mind.

01:24:27.370 --> 01:24:33.710
It’s your basic mammalian wiring, and you've
been working on that ever since we’ve had

01:24:33.710 --> 01:24:34.710
nervous systems.

01:24:34.710 --> 01:24:36.600
It’s a very long period of time.

01:24:36.600 --> 01:24:37.830
That sets you up.

01:24:37.830 --> 01:24:44.930
Now you have to add complexity to that, right,
because when you're trying to - because those

01:24:44.930 --> 01:24:50.940
systems only have a finite time range so when
you're angry, for example, you're angry right

01:24:50.940 --> 01:24:55.870
now and in fact part of a utility of angry
is that it’s right now because it’s a

01:24:55.870 --> 01:24:59.670
problem solving mechanism for dealing with
some obstacle that you can't get rid of any

01:24:59.670 --> 01:25:03.640
other way than to say, “To hell with the
future,” roughly speaking and deal with

01:25:03.640 --> 01:25:04.640
it right now.

01:25:04.640 --> 01:25:09.360
Now it’s dangerous, obviously, but it’s
also necessary because otherwise maybe you

01:25:09.360 --> 01:25:13.620
get locked into an argument, and you just
stand there arguing for the rest of your life,

01:25:13.620 --> 01:25:14.620
you know.

01:25:14.620 --> 01:25:17.620
At some point you say, I’m not talking to
you anymore, and the anger does that.

01:25:17.620 --> 01:25:19.780
It makes you impulsive and you can walk away.

01:25:19.780 --> 01:25:22.420
You know, and sometimes that’s the right
conclusion to an argument.

01:25:22.420 --> 01:25:27.160
You know, you're not going to win, and you
don't want to keep arguing so why do you stop?

01:25:27.160 --> 01:25:28.160
You get annoyed.

01:25:28.160 --> 01:25:29.160
It’s a solution.

01:25:29.160 --> 01:25:32.410
You've got all these basic biological systems.

01:25:32.410 --> 01:25:37.230
Now obviously, no one of them has the answer,
and you know that because if it did, you wouldn't

01:25:37.230 --> 01:25:39.890
need a bunch of them, and you have a whole
bunch of them.

01:25:39.890 --> 01:25:45.380
You have pain, and anxiety, and exploration,
and play, and sexuality, and hunger, and thirst,

01:25:45.380 --> 01:25:51.090
and you know you can come up with probably
twelve basic emotions and motivational systems.

01:25:51.090 --> 01:25:56.030
Okay, so you need all twelve of those in order
to orient yourselves properly in the world

01:25:56.030 --> 01:26:00.310
just like you need your five senses, but then
another problem starts to emerge and that

01:26:00.310 --> 01:26:07.490
problem is okay, those things have to be regulated
properly, but they have to be regulated across

01:26:07.490 --> 01:26:13.600
multiple spans of time, and then they have
to be regulated across multiple different

01:26:13.600 --> 01:26:15.570
complexities of social environment.

01:26:15.570 --> 01:26:18.020
So you don't just have one system of twelve.

01:26:18.020 --> 01:26:24.780
Now, you have a very large number of systems
of twelve for very long spans of time, so

01:26:24.780 --> 01:26:28.400
then you think, well out of that emerges a
solution.

01:26:28.400 --> 01:26:30.560
We talked about already with the evolution
of games.

01:26:30.560 --> 01:26:32.510
Out of that emerges a solution.

01:26:32.510 --> 01:26:35.250
You might think about those as higher order
meanings.

01:26:35.250 --> 01:26:39.030
So, and you know that, and you do it and you
believe it.

01:26:39.030 --> 01:26:40.760
That’s the thing that’s cool.

01:26:40.760 --> 01:26:42.710
Here you are in university.

01:26:42.710 --> 01:26:44.280
Well you ask yourself why?

01:26:44.280 --> 01:26:49.350
You might think, well what do you have to
believe in order to be university?

01:26:49.350 --> 01:26:54.990
Well, at some point, your beliefs run out
and you're just here because you've made that

01:26:54.990 --> 01:26:55.990
decision.

01:26:55.990 --> 01:26:58.140
But I can ask you, you know, why are you in
university?

01:26:58.140 --> 01:27:02.730
And most people have about five reasons, and
then they don't know what to say.

01:27:02.730 --> 01:27:07.810
So what that means is they have reason A,
and reason B, and reason C, and reason D,

01:27:07.810 --> 01:27:12.190
and then they're just acting it out after
that, and if you keep questioning them, they

01:27:12.190 --> 01:27:14.710
get perturbed and they'll get annoyed.

01:27:14.710 --> 01:27:15.960
Sometimes they'll cry.

01:27:15.960 --> 01:27:18.730
Sometimes they'll get angry, like if you're
really going after someone, it’s like what

01:27:18.730 --> 01:27:20.580
the hell are you up to and why?

01:27:20.580 --> 01:27:24.070
You know, sometimes you have to do that to
untangle something very complex.

01:27:24.070 --> 01:27:28.400
At some point, people just - they get angry
or they cry, and that means to hell with you

01:27:28.400 --> 01:27:32.250
or you're bullying me, leave me alone, and
that's supposed to get rid of you, you know,

01:27:32.250 --> 01:27:36.170
but it doesn't work if you really want to
get down to the answers.

01:27:36.170 --> 01:27:39.820
Well, partly what we’re trying to figure
out is why the hell are you in university?

01:27:39.820 --> 01:27:43.150
You might say, “Well, to get a job,” but
I might say, “Well why in the world would

01:27:43.150 --> 01:27:44.950
that make you satisfied with life?”

01:27:44.950 --> 01:27:49.250
Why do you think that would make you satisfied
with life, and you might say, “Well it’s

01:27:49.250 --> 01:27:50.420
better than not having a job.”

01:27:50.420 --> 01:27:54.780
It’s like yeah yeah fair enough, you know,
like right let’s not forget the practical,

01:27:54.780 --> 01:27:57.100
but it isn't the solution.

01:27:57.100 --> 01:28:02.920
It’s part of a solution, and the question
is do you really believe there's a solution?

01:28:02.920 --> 01:28:07.730
Well you must because you believe it enough
so that you're here, and then you believe

01:28:07.730 --> 01:28:12.380
that has something to do with the ability
to think - and you believe that has something

01:28:12.380 --> 01:28:16.190
to do with the necessity of understanding
your history and you believe that it has something

01:28:16.190 --> 01:28:23.040
to do with the utility of exposing yourself
to what in principle are great rate historical

01:28:23.040 --> 01:28:24.770
and literary works.

01:28:24.770 --> 01:28:29.850
You assume that that's all valid because even
if it’s implicitly, if it’s implicitly,

01:28:29.850 --> 01:28:34.420
it means you're imitating someone who assumes
all that, but either way here you are.

01:28:34.420 --> 01:28:37.780
You're doing something, and the questions
is - and maybe this is the critical, I think

01:28:37.780 --> 01:28:41.960
this was the critical question of the 20th
century.

01:28:41.960 --> 01:28:45.130
Is there any reality to what you are doing,
or is it just arbitrary?

01:28:45.130 --> 01:28:51.920
If it’s arbitrary, well you know that's
a path to nihilism because who cares what

01:28:51.920 --> 01:28:53.690
you do?

01:28:53.690 --> 01:28:55.150
Whatever you do, you're ruined.

01:28:55.150 --> 01:28:57.690
You can make a strong case for that, you know.

01:28:57.690 --> 01:29:03.330
But the problem with that seems to be nihilism
- try it for a while, you know.

01:29:03.330 --> 01:29:09.900
What it produces is misery, and people generally
believe in misery, and they generally believe

01:29:09.900 --> 01:29:13.680
that it’s not a good thing to have, and
so I would say nihilism is a self defeating

01:29:13.680 --> 01:29:18.960
proposition if you assume that misery is actually
a source of valid information, and you all

01:29:18.960 --> 01:29:24.160
do because you know, if someone is jabbing
you with a pen, you're going to move you arm

01:29:24.160 --> 01:29:28.500
because you feel that if you can avoid some
misery, that's a good thing.

01:29:28.500 --> 01:29:31.640
So you have faith in your experience at that
level.

01:29:31.640 --> 01:29:37.110
Well then you might say well you know, if
you can really make a solid case for the believability

01:29:37.110 --> 01:29:41.820
for certain kinds of experiences, that they're
real, why can't you make a case for the believability

01:29:41.820 --> 01:29:43.800
for higher forms of meaning?

01:29:43.800 --> 01:29:45.480
Maybe they're not arbitrary.

01:29:45.480 --> 01:29:50.000
We don't know, but I tell you if you act like
they're arbitrary, you get into trouble very,

01:29:50.000 --> 01:29:55.300
very rapidly because you either become nihilistic
or halfway down the trip to nihilism, you

01:29:55.300 --> 01:30:00.670
adopt some horrible ideology and then you
know, you're possessed by it, and then you're

01:30:00.670 --> 01:30:05.530
well - then you're not going to live your
own life because you're going to live the

01:30:05.530 --> 01:30:09.380
life of the ideology and you're going to be
dangerous to everyone around you and also

01:30:09.380 --> 01:30:12.260
impervious to any sort of reasonable communication.

01:30:12.260 --> 01:30:17.320
So if those are the two consequences of making
the presumption of arbitrary meaning at a

01:30:17.320 --> 01:30:20.591
higher order level, then to me, they've invalidated
themselves already.

01:30:20.591 --> 01:30:22.920
We’ve already had that experiment.

01:30:22.920 --> 01:30:24.030
It didn't work.

01:30:24.030 --> 01:30:26.840
And so then the question is well what's next?

01:30:26.840 --> 01:30:32.710
Well that’s partly what we’re trying to
figure out right now.

